\par \pard \page #{\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 # Open_Watcom_Integrated_Development_Environment}${\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 $ Open Watcom Integrated Development Environment}!{\footnote ! ChangeButtonBinding( "btn_up", "Contents()") }K{\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 K ;Integrated Development Environment}+{\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 + Open Watcom Integrated Development Environment:0001}{\keepn \f2 \fs28 Open Watcom Integrated Development Environment \f2 \fs20 \par \pard \f2 \fs20 \tx360 \tx720 \tx1080 \tx1440 \tx1800 \tx2160 \tx2520 \tx2880 \tx3240 \tx3600 \tx3960 \tx4320 \tx4680 \tx5040 \tx5400 }
\par Software development normally involves managing a number of different files for each project.  A project often includes multiple source files, include files, and libraries.  In addition, there are usually several programming tools involved:  compilers, linkers, preprocessors, debuggers, editors, and so on.  But this is likely not new to you.  What will be new is the Open Watcom Integrated Development Environment (IDE).
\par 
\par The Open Watcom Integrated Development Environment is a software development tool that manages project files and language tools.  Although it is really a tool itself, the common term to describe it (and possibly the best analogy) is\b  Development Environment.\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0   By using the Open Watcom Integrated Development Environment, you have direct access to each of the files relating to a project and all of the language tools you require to build your application.
\par 
\par \qc \{bmc ide_s.bmp\}
\par \pard \page #{\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 # How_the_Environment_Operates}${\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 $ How the Environment Operates}!{\footnote ! ChangeButtonBinding( "btn_up", "JumpId( `ide.hlp', `Open_Watcom_Integrated_Development_Environment')") }K{\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 K ;Integrated Development Environment, environment}+{\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 + Open Watcom Integrated Development Environment:0002}{\keepn \f2 \fs28 How the Environment Operates \f2 \fs20 \par \pard \f2 \fs20 \tx360 \tx720 \tx1080 \tx1440 \tx1800 \tx2160 \tx2520 \tx2880 \tx3240 \tx3600 \tx3960 \tx4320 \tx4680 \tx5040 \tx5400 }
\par The Open Watcom Integrated Development Environment has a graphical interface that combines file management and tool management.  An IDE session deals with a\b  Project.\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0   A project can be generally defined as the collection of files and necessary information that relates to the development of project components.  However, a project is more than just a graphical representation of a file list-it contains all of the underlying smarts to understand the interrelation of all files associated with the project and to ensure that these files are up-to-date.
\par 
\par Each project component is referred to as a\b  Target.\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0   An Integrated Development Environment target is made up of the source files, include files, libraries, and MAKE instructions to create the target file (for example, ".exe", ".dll", ".lib", or ".hlp").  A project often has more than one target.  Each target can be set to create the associated target file for a specific destination platform.
\par 
\par Within a target, there are\b  Sources.\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0   A source is an individual file (such as a ".c" or ".cpp" file, but it can also be a ".bmp", ".rc", or ".lib" file) used to create the target file.  A target usually has more than one source.
\par \pard \page #{\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 # How_the_Integrated_Development_Environment_Looks}${\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 $ How the Integrated Development Environment Looks}!{\footnote ! ChangeButtonBinding( "btn_up", "JumpId( `ide.hlp', `Open_Watcom_Integrated_Development_Environment')") }K{\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 K ;look and feel of the Integrated Development Environment}+{\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 + Open Watcom Integrated Development Environment:0003}{\keepn \f2 \fs28 How the Integrated Development Environment Looks \f2 \fs20 \par \pard \f2 \fs20 \tx360 \tx720 \tx1080 \tx1440 \tx1800 \tx2160 \tx2520 \tx2880 \tx3240 \tx3600 \tx3960 \tx4320 \tx4680 \tx5040 \tx5400 }
\par In the following illustration, the main window is the project window for a project entitled\f4 \fs24  first.wpj.\f2 \fs20   The sub-window represents the target for this project,\f4 \fs24  target1.exe.\f2 \fs20   The source file for the target is listed within the target window.  In this project, there is only a single target with a single source file.
\par 
\par \f4 \fs24 \qc \{bmc ide1.bmp\}\f2 \fs20 
\par \ql \i \b Figure 1.\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0 \i \b \b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0 \b  The Open Watcom IDE window displays project and target windows.\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0 
\par 
\par The language tools are available on the toolbar and the drop-down menus.  For example, on the Sources menu, you can work with the source that is selected in the target window.  Here you can add a new source, remove or rename an existing source, edit a source, and so on.  A similar set of target actions are available in the Targets menu and project actions are located in the File menu.
\par 
\par A single IDE session displays a project.  If the project consists of a number of components, such as two executables and one library, these appear as target sub-windows on the project window.  Each target window displays the files necessary to construct the target and associates files with a rule describing the construction mechanism based on filename extension.  For example, a filename with the extension ".exe" may associate with the rule for constructing executables, or a filename with the extension ".lib" may associate with the rule for constructing static libraries.
\par 
\par Different projects can refer to the same target.  If they do, either project can manipulate the shared target and any changes to either project affect the shared target.
\par \pard \page #{\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 # The_Files_the_Integrated_Development_Environment_Uses}${\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 $ The Files the Integrated Development Environment Uses}!{\footnote ! ChangeButtonBinding( "btn_up", "JumpId( `ide.hlp', `Open_Watcom_Integrated_Development_Environment')") }K{\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 K ;projects}+{\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 + Open Watcom Integrated Development Environment:0004}{\keepn \f2 \fs28 The Files the Integrated Development Environment Uses \f2 \fs20 \par \pard \f2 \fs20 \tx360 \tx720 \tx1080 \tx1440 \tx1800 \tx2160 \tx2520 \tx2880 \tx3240 \tx3600 \tx3960 \tx4320 \tx4680 \tx5040 \tx5400 }
\par The Open Watcom Integrated Development Environment creates and manages a project, referencing a number of targets.  The IDE uses filename extensions to represent projects and targets.  The extension ".wpj" represents projects and the extension ".tgt" represents targets.  The IDE compiles and links the target in the target directory.
\par 
\par The project file (.wpj) contains the names of all of the target files related to the project, including housekeeping information about the project and the current IDE session.
\par 
\par Each target file (.tgt) describes one target and includes the name and type of the target executable, a reference to a rule describing how to build the target, and a list of relevant switches the rule uses.  In addition, target files also contain the names of all of the source files used to construct the target program, references to similar rules for compiling them, and relevant switches.
\par 
\par In constructing targets, the Integrated Development Environment references a list of rules defined in a configuration file, called\f4 \fs24  ide.cfg.\f2 \fs20   Each rule defines a command line used to do the construction and a list of actions you can perform on the target program (such as make, run, or debug) or on the files in the item list (such as make or edit).  Additionally, rules refer to tools.
\par 
\par Tools define which command line switches you can use in the Open Watcom Integrated Development Environment.
\par \pard \page #{\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 # Using_the_Open_Watcom_Integrated_Development_Environment}${\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 $ Using the Open Watcom Integrated Development Environment}!{\footnote ! ChangeButtonBinding( "btn_up", "JumpId( `ide.hlp', `Open_Watcom_Integrated_Development_Environment')") }K{\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 K ;Integrated Development Environment, using the}+{\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 + Open Watcom Integrated Development Environment:0005}{\keepn \f2 \fs28 Using the Open Watcom Integrated Development Environment \f2 \fs20 \par \pard \f2 \fs20 \tx360 \tx720 \tx1080 \tx1440 \tx1800 \tx2160 \tx2520 \tx2880 \tx3240 \tx3600 \tx3960 \tx4320 \tx4680 \tx5040 \tx5400 }
\par This section discusses the following topics:
\par 
\par \li360 \fi-360\f1 \'b7\f2\tab Starting the Open Watcom Integrated Development Environment
\par \fi0 \fi-360\f1 \'b7\f2\tab Quitting the Open Watcom Integrated Development Environment
\par \fi0 \fi-360\f1 \'b7\f2\tab The IDE Menu Bar
\par \fi0 \fi-360\f1 \'b7\f2\tab The IDE Status Bar
\par \fi0 \fi-360\f1 \'b7\f2\tab The IDE Toolbar
\par \pard \page #{\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 # Starting_the_Open_Watcom_Integrated_Development_Environment}${\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 $ Starting the Open Watcom Integrated Development Environment}!{\footnote ! ChangeButtonBinding( "btn_up", "JumpId( `ide.hlp', `Using_the_Open_Watcom_Integrated_Development_Environment')") }K{\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 K ;Integrated Development Environment, starting the}+{\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 + Open Watcom Integrated Development Environment:0006}{\keepn \f2 \fs28 Starting the Open Watcom Integrated Development Environment \f2 \fs20 \par \pard \f2 \fs20 \tx360 \tx720 \tx1080 \tx1440 \tx1800 \tx2160 \tx2520 \tx2880 \tx3240 \tx3600 \tx3960 \tx4320 \tx4680 \tx5040 \tx5400 }
\par To start the Integrated Development Environment, double-click on the IDE icon.  This opens the Open Watcom IDE Project window.
\par \pard \page #{\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 # Quitting_the_Open_Watcom_Integrated_Development_Environment}${\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 $ Quitting the Open Watcom Integrated Development Environment}!{\footnote ! ChangeButtonBinding( "btn_up", "JumpId( `ide.hlp', `Using_the_Open_Watcom_Integrated_Development_Environment')") }K{\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 K ;Integrated Development Environment, quitting the}+{\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 + Open Watcom Integrated Development Environment:0007}{\keepn \f2 \fs28 Quitting the Open Watcom Integrated Development Environment \f2 \fs20 \par \pard \f2 \fs20 \tx360 \tx720 \tx1080 \tx1440 \tx1800 \tx2160 \tx2520 \tx2880 \tx3240 \tx3600 \tx3960 \tx4320 \tx4680 \tx5040 \tx5400 }
\par To exit the Integrated Development Environment, choose Exit from the File menu of the Open Watcom IDE Project window or double-click on the system menu box on the same window.
\par \pard \page #{\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 # The_IDE_Menu_Bar}${\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 $ The IDE Menu Bar}!{\footnote ! ChangeButtonBinding( "btn_up", "JumpId( `ide.hlp', `Using_the_Open_Watcom_Integrated_Development_Environment')") }K{\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 K ;menu bar, Integrated Development Environment;Integrated Development Environment, menu bar}+{\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 + Open Watcom Integrated Development Environment:0008}{\keepn \f2 \fs28 The IDE Menu Bar \f2 \fs20 \par \pard \f2 \fs20 \tx360 \tx720 \tx1080 \tx1440 \tx1800 \tx2160 \tx2520 \tx2880 \tx3240 \tx3600 \tx3960 \tx4320 \tx4680 \tx5040 \tx5400 }
\par The IDE's menu bar consists of the following eight menus:
\par 
\par \li360 \f2\fi-360 \b File\plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 Open new or existing projects, close and save projects, exit the IDE, and show or hide the toolbar and status bar
\par 
\par \f2\fi-360 \b Actions\plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 Make all targets, run applications, and invoke other GUI Tools
\par 
\par \f2\fi-360 \b Targets\plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 Work with targets, including setting switches and creating, removing, and renaming targets
\par 
\par \f2\fi-360 \b Sources\plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 Work with sources, including setting switches and creating, removing, and renaming sources
\par 
\par \f2\fi-360 \b Options\plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 Set switches for creating targets and sources
\par 
\par \f2\fi-360 \b Log\plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 Perform functions relating to the IDE Log window
\par 
\par \f2\fi-360 \b Window\plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 Refresh the contents of the IDE windows and view the targets currently available on the IDE window
\par 
\par \f2\fi-360 \b Help\plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 Access on-line help information
\par \pard \page #{\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 # The_IDE_Status_Bar}${\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 $ The IDE Status Bar}!{\footnote ! ChangeButtonBinding( "btn_up", "JumpId( `ide.hlp', `Using_the_Open_Watcom_Integrated_Development_Environment')") }K{\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 K ;status bar, Integrated Development Environment;Integrated Development Environment, status bar}+{\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 + Open Watcom Integrated Development Environment:0009}{\keepn \f2 \fs28 The IDE Status Bar \f2 \fs20 \par \pard \f2 \fs20 \tx360 \tx720 \tx1080 \tx1440 \tx1800 \tx2160 \tx2520 \tx2880 \tx3240 \tx3600 \tx3960 \tx4320 \tx4680 \tx5040 \tx5400 }
\par The Status Bar appears along the bottom of the Open Watcom IDE Project window.  This bar displays hint text for the toolbar button and menu option over which your cursor is positioned.  Status messages, such as Saving, Adding, and Executing Log, appear in this bar so you are aware of what action the IDE is performing.  A check mark beside the Show Status Bar menu option in the File menu indicates that this feature is enabled.  To disable this feature, choose the option again from the File menu and the status bar disappears from the window.
\par 
\par \f4 \fs24 \qc \{bmc ide2.bmp\}\f2 \fs20 
\par \ql \i \b Figure 2.\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0 \i \b \b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0 \b  The IDE Status Bar can be toggled from the File menu.\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0 
\par \pard \page #{\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 # The_IDE_Toolbar}${\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 $ The IDE Toolbar}!{\footnote ! ChangeButtonBinding( "btn_up", "JumpId( `ide.hlp', `Using_the_Open_Watcom_Integrated_Development_Environment')") }K{\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 K ;make all targets;profile target;sample target;browse target;remote debug target;debug target;run target;make target;make source;edit text;save project;open project;new project;tool bar, Integrated Development Environment;Integrated Development Environment, tool bar}+{\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 + Open Watcom Integrated Development Environment:0010}{\keepn \f2 \fs28 The IDE Toolbar \f2 \fs20 \par \pard \f2 \fs20 \tx360 \tx720 \tx1080 \tx1440 \tx1800 \tx2160 \tx2520 \tx2880 \tx3240 \tx3600 \tx3960 \tx4320 \tx4680 \tx5040 \tx5400 }
\par In the Open Watcom IDE Project window, the toolbar appears below the menu bar when the Show Tool Bar option in the File menu is activated (default).  A check mark beside the menu option indicates that it is enabled.  To disable this feature, choose the option again from the File menu and the toolbar disappears.
\par 
\par \f4 \fs24 \qc \{bmc ide3.bmp\}\f2 \fs20 
\par \ql \i \b Figure 3.\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0 \i \b \b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0 \b  You can access thirteen IDE functions from the toolbar.\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0 
\par 
\par The following explains the function each icon performs, as well as the equivalent function on the menu bar.
\par 
\par \li360 \f2\fi-360 \b Create\plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 \{bmc newproj.bmp\} Create a new project.  This is equivalent to the option New Project in the File menu.
\par 
\par \f2\fi-360 \b Open\plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 \{bmc openproj.bmp\} Open an existing project.  This is equivalent to the option Open Project in the File menu.
\par 
\par \f2\fi-360 \b Save\plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 \{bmc saveproj.bmp\} Save the current project.  This is equivalent to the option Save Project in the File menu.
\par 
\par \f2\fi-360 \b Edit\plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 \{bmc edititem.bmp\} Edit the selected source file.  This is equivalent to the option Edit Text in the Sources menu.
\par 
\par \f2\fi-360 \b Make Source\plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 \{bmc makeitem.bmp\} Make the selected source file.  This is equivalent to the option Make in the Sources menu.
\par 
\par \f2\fi-360 \b Make Target\plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 \{bmc maketarg.bmp\} Make the current target.  This is equivalent to the option Make in the Targets menu.
\par 
\par \f2\fi-360 \b Run\plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 \{bmc runtarg.bmp\} Run the current target.  This is equivalent to the option Run in the Targets menu.
\par 
\par \f2\fi-360 \b Debug\plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 \{bmc debgtarg.bmp\} Debug the current target.  This is equivalent to the option Debug in the Targets menu.
\par 
\par \f2\fi-360 \b Remote Debug\plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 \{bmc rdbgtarg.bmp\} Debug the current target on a remote machine.  This is equivalent to the option Remote Debug in the Targets menu.
\par 
\par \f2\fi-360 \b Browse\plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 \{bmc browtarg.bmp\} Browse the current target.  This is equivalent to the option Browse in the Targets menu.  Currently, browsing is supported by the Open Watcom C/C++ compilers only (Open Watcom FORTRAN 77 does not support browsing).
\par 
\par \f2\fi-360 \b Sample\plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 \{bmc samptarg.bmp\} Run and sample the current target.  This is equivalent to the option Sample in the Targets menu.
\par 
\par \f2\fi-360 \b Profile\plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 \{bmc proftarg.bmp\} Profile the current target.  This is equivalent to the option Profile in the Targets menu.
\par 
\par \f2\fi-360 \b Make All\plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 \{bmc makeproj.bmp\} Make all targets in the project.  This is equivalent to the option Make All in the Actions menu.
\par \pard \page #{\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 # Getting_Started_with_a_Short_Tutorial}${\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 $ Getting Started with a Short Tutorial}!{\footnote ! ChangeButtonBinding( "btn_up", "JumpId( `ide.hlp', `Open_Watcom_Integrated_Development_Environment')") }K{\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 K ;tutorial, Integrated Development Environment;Integrated Development Environment, tutorial}+{\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 + Open Watcom Integrated Development Environment:0011}{\keepn \f2 \fs28 Getting Started with a Short Tutorial \f2 \fs20 \par \pard \f2 \fs20 \tx360 \tx720 \tx1080 \tx1440 \tx1800 \tx2160 \tx2520 \tx2880 \tx3240 \tx3600 \tx3960 \tx4320 \tx4680 \tx5040 \tx5400 }
\par Perhaps the easiest way to understand how the Open Watcom Integrated Development Environment manages an entire project's files and tools, is to work through a brief example.  In this walk-through, you create and execute a simple C program using the IDE.  FORTRAN developers can follow along substituting FORTRAN code and file names where appropriate.  The session will help you understand the basic concepts of the IDE and the steps involved in project development.
\par 
\par The illustrations shown in this section are taken from the Windows environment.  The OS/2 environment is very similar with some minor differences.  For example, in the OS/2 environment the IDE uses the OS/2 Editor rather than Open Watcom's Windows Editor.  Although this tutorial builds a Win32 executable, you can choose to build a Win16 or OS/2 PM executable.  The procedures to follow are not that much different.  When the time comes to select the type of target, simply choose an environment that you can run on your machine so that you can run your application to make sure it works.
\par \pard \page #{\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 # A_Simple_Executable}${\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 $ A Simple Executable}!{\footnote ! ChangeButtonBinding( "btn_up", "JumpId( `ide.hlp', `Getting_Started_with_a_Short_Tutorial')") }K{\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 K ;save project;auto refresh;new project;Integrated Development Environment, defining a project}+{\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 + Open Watcom Integrated Development Environment:0012}{\keepn \f2 \fs28 A Simple Executable \f2 \fs20 \par \pard \f2 \fs20 \tx360 \tx720 \tx1080 \tx1440 \tx1800 \tx2160 \tx2520 \tx2880 \tx3240 \tx3600 \tx3960 \tx4320 \tx4680 \tx5040 \tx5400 }
\par \li360 \f2\fi-360 \b (1)\plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 \b  Double-click on the IDE icon\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  in the Open Watcom Tools window.
\par 
\par The Open Watcom IDE Project window appears.  
\par 
\par \f2\fi-360 \b (2)\plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 \b  Choose New Project\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  from the File menu.
\par 
\par The Enter Project Filename dialog appears.  The default project name is\f4 \fs24  noname.wpj.\f2 \fs20 
\par 
\par \f2\fi-360 \b (3)\plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 \b  Enter the filename\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0 \f4 \fs24  \\project\\first\f2 \fs20  in the File Name field and click on OK.
\par 
\par \f4 \fs24 \qc \{bmc ide4.bmp\}\f2 \fs20 
\par \ql \i \b Figure 4.\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0 \i \b \b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0 \b  Enter the filename in the Enter Project Filename dialog.\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0 
\par 
\par This is the name of the project you are creating.  If you enter a directory that doesn't exist (in this case,\f4 \fs24  \\project\f2 \fs20 ), the IDE reveals a message box asking if you want to create the specified directory.  Click on OK to create the specified directory and project file.
\par 
\par \li720 \f2\fi-360 \b Note:\plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 The IDE looks for existing projects with the new name.  If a project with the specified name already exists, the IDE will prompt you rather than simply overwriting the existing project.
\par \li360\fi0 
\par The project name appears in the title bar of the Open Watcom IDE Project window.  Note that the IDE added the extension ".wpj" to the project name.  The New Target dialog appears on the screen where you define a target for the new project.
\par 
\par \f2\fi-360 \b (4)\plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 \b  Enter the desired target name\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  in the Target Name field (we suggest\f4 \fs24  target1\f2 \fs20 ).  Next, select the appropriate Target Environment (we suggest "Win32") and Image Type (we suggest "Character-mode Executable").  Click on OK when completed.
\par 
\par \f4 \fs24 \qc \{bmc ide5.bmp\}\f2 \fs20 
\par \ql \i \b Figure 5.\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0 \i \b \b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0 \b  Select target name, target environment and image type\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0 
\par 
\par The New Target dialog closes and the IDE creates a target window entitled\f4 \fs24  target1.exe\f2 \fs20  for the new target.  Note that the IDE appends the appropriate extension to the target name for you once you click on OK.  You can now add sources to the target.
\par 
\par \f2\fi-360 \b (5)\plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 \b  Choose New Source\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  from the Sources menu.
\par 
\par The Add File(s) dialog appears where you specify the sources you want to add to the target.  Source names must be filenames with extensions; pathnames are not required.
\par 
\par For the OS/2 IDE, you can add multiple source files to the target at one time by inserting a space between each filename you enter.  You can also use the file browser to add files by clicking on the Browse...  button.
\par 
\par For all other systems, you can enter the names of the source files (with extensions) in the File Name field or you can use the file browser to select files.  The type of files displayed in file list window can be controlled by modifying the List Files of Type field.  Once you have typed a name in the File Name field or selected a name from the file list, you can add it to the list of source files to be included by clicking the Add button.  All files in the file list can be added by clicking the Add All button.  Individual names can be removed from the "add" list by selecting them and clicking the Remove button.
\par 
\par \f4 \fs24 \qc \{bmc ide6.bmp\}\f2 \fs20 
\par \ql \i \b Figure 6.\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0 \i \b \b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0 \b  Select source file names in the Add File(s) dialog\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0 
\par 
\par \f2\fi-360 \b (6)\plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 \b  Enter the following source file names.\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0 
\par 
\par \f4 \fs24       \f2 \fs20 
\par \f4 \fs24      source1.c   (or source1.for)\f2 \fs20 
\par \f4 \fs24      source2.c   (or source2.for)\f2 \fs20 
\par \f4 \fs24      source3.rc\f2 \fs20 
\par 
\par For the OS/2 IDE, enter all the names into the field separating each name by a space.  Click on OK when done.
\par 
\par For all other systems, enter one name at a time into the File Name field, pressing Enter or clicking on Add after each one has been entered.  Click on Close when done.
\par 
\par \f4 \fs24 \qc \{bmc ide7.bmp\}\f2 \fs20 
\par \ql \i \b Figure 7.\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0 \i \b \b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0 \b  Enter one or several source files in the Add File(s) dialog.\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0 
\par 
\par The IDE adds the new source files to the target window, followed by the characters [n/a].  This indicates that the source files are not available, meaning that the files do not yet exist.  The indicators are:
\par 
\par \li720 \f2\fi-360 \b [n/a]\plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 This code appears if the file does not exist.
\par 
\par \f2\fi-360 \b [r/o]\plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 This code appears if the file is Read Only.
\par 
\par \f2\fi-360 \b [sw]\plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 This code appears if the user has set switches for the specified source file.
\par \li360\fi0 
\par The Integrated Development Environment organizes the source files in the target window under headers according to the filename extension.  You can expand and collapse the file groups by clicking on the folder icon.
\par 
\par For this tutorial, we only need one source file, so now we will delete the extra files from the target.
\par 
\par \f2\fi-360 \b (7)\plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 \b  Click on the file\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0 \f4 \fs24  source2.c\f2 \fs20  (or\f4 \fs24  source2.for\f2 \fs20 ) to select it then choose Remove Source from the Sources menu.
\par 
\par A message box appears asking if you want to remove the selected source.  Click on the Yes button to continue.  The IDE removes the selected source file from the target window.  Now we can remove\f4 \fs24  source3.rc.\f2 \fs20   To do this we will perform the function of removing all source files with a common extension.
\par 
\par \f2\fi-360 \b (8)\plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 \b  Click on the (.rc) header\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  on the target window then choose Remove Source from the Sources menu.
\par 
\par A message box appears on the screen asking if you want to remove all of the ".rc" files.  Click on the Yes button to continue.  The message box closes and the IDE removes the\f4 \fs24  source3.rc\f2 \fs20  file from the target window.  Now you can edit the remaining source file.
\par 
\par \f2\fi-360 \b (9)\plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 \b  Highlight the line\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0 \f4 \fs24  source1.c\f2 \fs20  (or\f4 \fs24  source1.for\f2 \fs20 ) and choose Edit Text from the Sources menu to edit the selected source file.
\par 
\par The Open Watcom Editor appears.  The name of the selected source file,\f4 \fs24  source1.c\f2 \fs20  (or\f4 \fs24  source1.for\f2 \fs20 ), appears in the window.  Here you can enter the source code.
\par 
\par \i \b Hint:\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0   You can also invoke the Open Watcom Editor by double-clicking on the source file that you want to edit.
\par 
\par In this case, the Open Watcom Editor appears because the selected source file has a ".c" or ".for" extension.  Performing an edit can open different editors, depending on the extension of the selected source file.
\par 
\par \f2\fi-360 \b (10)\plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 \b  Enter the following lines of source code.\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0 
\par 
\par \li720 \f2\fi-360 \b Important:\plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 For the purpose of this session, we ask you to enter\b  ii\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  instead of\b  i\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  in the "print" line.  This will allow you to see how the Integrated Development Environment reacts to errors.  You will then be able to correct the error.
\par \li360\fi0 
\par \f4 \fs24       \f2 \fs20 
\par \f4 \fs24      #include <stdio.h>\f2 \fs20 
\par 
\par \f4 \fs24      void main()\f2 \fs20 
\par \f4 \fs24      \{\f2 \fs20 
\par \f4 \fs24          int i;\f2 \fs20 
\par \f4 \fs24          for (i=0; i<10; i++)\f2 \fs20 
\par \f4 \fs24          \{\f2 \fs20 
\par \f4 \fs24              printf( "Value is %d\\n", ii );\f2 \fs20 
\par \f4 \fs24          \}\f2 \fs20 
\par \f4 \fs24          printf("Press Enter...\\n");\f2 \fs20 
\par \f4 \fs24          getchar();\f2 \fs20 
\par \f4 \fs24      \}\f2 \fs20 
\par 
\par FORTRAN programmers can enter the following lines of code.
\par 
\par \f4 \fs24       \f2 \fs20 
\par \f4 \fs24            program main\f2 \fs20 
\par \f4 \fs24            implicit none\f2 \fs20 
\par \f4 \fs24            integer i\f2 \fs20 
\par 
\par \f4 \fs24            do i = 0, 9\f2 \fs20 
\par \f4 \fs24              print *, 'Value is ', ii\f2 \fs20 
\par \f4 \fs24            enddo\f2 \fs20 
\par 
\par \f4 \fs24            print *, 'Press Enter...'\f2 \fs20 
\par \f4 \fs24            read *\f2 \fs20 
\par 
\par \f4 \fs24            end\f2 \fs20 
\par 
\par You can now save your work and close the Editor in one of the following two ways:  close down the entire editor or close down only the source file window.  For this exercise, close only the source file window as follows.
\par 
\par \f2\fi-360 \b (11)\plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 \b  Choose Close\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  from the File menu to close down the current source file window.
\par 
\par In both cases, if changes have been made to the file and not saved, a message prompts you to save the changes before exiting.  The advantage of closing only the Editor window is that the Editor continues to run.  If you need to access it again, you will not need to wait for it to go through the start-up procedures again.
\par 
\par \li720 \f2\fi-360 \b Important:\plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 You must save the file.  The Open Watcom compilers do not access the source lines in the Editor's buffers.
\par \li360\fi0 
\par \f2\fi-360 \b (12)\plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 \b  Minimize the Editor window.\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0 
\par 
\par or
\par 
\par \f2\fi-360 \b \plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 \b Click on the Open Watcom IDE Project window\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  to bring it to the foreground.
\par 
\par \f2\fi-360 \b (13)\plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 \b  Choose Refresh\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  from the Window menu.
\par 
\par This action forces the IDE to update the display with the state of all files.  It is not necessary to use this function if you have selected Auto Refresh in the File menu.  If Auto Refresh is disabled then it may be necessary to alert the IDE of the presence of the file on disk.  
\par 
\par \f2\fi-360 \b (14)\plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 \b  Choose Save Project\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  from the File menu to save your project up to this point.
\par 
\par You are now ready to make and run the target.  Making a target is the act of constructing a target file.  In this case, it involves compiling the source file\f4 \fs24  source1.c\f2 \fs20  (or\f4 \fs24  source1.for\f2 \fs20 ) using the appropriate Open Watcom compiler and linking the target executable using the Open Watcom Linker.
\par 
\par \f2\fi-360 \b (15)\plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 \b  Highlight the line\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0 \f4 \fs24  source1.c\f2 \fs20  and choose C Compiler Switches...  from the Source Options item located under the Sources menu to set compile options.
\par 
\par \b FORTRAN programmers:\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0   Highlight the line\f4 \fs24  source1.for\f2 \fs20  and choose Fortran Compiler Switches...  from the Source Options item located under the Sources menu to set compile options.
\par 
\par The C Compiler Switches dialog appears.  If you are using FORTRAN, the Fortran Compiler Switches dialog appears.
\par 
\par \f2\fi-360 \b (16)\plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 \b  Click on the ">>" button\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  until the\f4 \fs24  7.  CodeGen Strategy Switches\f2 \fs20  dialog appears.  Click on the "Multithreaded application [-bm]" checkbox button to select this option.
\par 
\par \b FORTRAN programmers:\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0   Click on the ">>" button until the\f4 \fs24  9.  Application Type Switches\f2 \fs20  dialog appears.  Click on the "Multithreaded application [-bm]" checkbox to select this option.
\par 
\par A check-mark appears in the checkbox.
\par 
\par Note:  Selecting this option is for demonstration purposes only, it is not required for this simple application.
\par 
\par Click OK to close the dialog.
\par 
\par \f2\fi-360 \b (17)\plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 \b  Choose Make\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  from the Targets menu.
\par 
\par The IDE constructs a makefile based on its knowledge of the target and construction rules and then invokes the Open Watcom Make utility to create\f4 \fs24  target1.exe.\f2 \fs20   The IDE Log window appears at the bottom of the Open Watcom IDE Project window.  Use this window to monitor the procedure.  Since you entered\b  ii\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  instead of\b  i,\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  the MAKE fails.
\par 
\par \f4 \fs24 \qc \{bmc ide8.bmp\}\f2 \fs20 
\par \ql \i \b Figure 8.\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0 \i \b \b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0 \b  Use the IDE Log window to monitor the make procedure.\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0 
\par 
\par \f2\fi-360 \b (18)\plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 \b  Highlight the error message\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  in the IDE Log window and choose Help on Message from the Log menu.
\par Help information regarding the error message appears on the screen.
\par 
\par \f4 \fs24 \qc \{bmc ide9.bmp\}\f2 \fs20 
\par \ql \i \b Figure 9.\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0 \i \b \b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0 \b  Help for error messages is available\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0 
\par 
\par Close the Help window and edit the source file as follows.
\par 
\par \f2\fi-360 \b (19)\plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 \b  Highlight the error message\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  in the IDE Log window and choose Edit File from the Log menu.
\par 
\par or
\par 
\par \f2\fi-360 \b \plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 \b Double-click on the error message\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  in the IDE Log window.
\par 
\par The Open Watcom Editor opens and the cursor appears at the position of the error.  Edit and correct the error and then save the file again.  Minimize the Editor window and bring the IDE window to the foreground.
\par 
\par \f2\fi-360 \b (20)\plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 \b  Choose Make\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  from the Targets menu again.
\par 
\par This time, the procedure creates the executable file\f4 \fs24  target1.exe.\f2 \fs20 
\par 
\par \f2\fi-360 \b (21)\plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 \b  Choose Run\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  from the Targets menu.
\par 
\par The IDE executes the program\f4 \fs24  target1.exe.\f2 \fs20 
\par 
\par \f4 \fs24 \qc \{bmc ide10.bmp\}\f2 \fs20 
\par \ql \i \b Figure 10.\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0 \i \b \b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0 \b  Run from the Targets menu runs the target.\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0 
\par 
\par The program you wrote is a character mode application and its output appears in a separate console window.  You are now ready to debug your target program.  You probably do not need to debug this simple program with the Open Watcom Debugger but go through the steps so that you know what to do for more complex programs.
\par 
\par The IDE has several debugging information options which it uses as defaults when it is started.  The first thing to do is check to make sure that your application compiled with the default debugging information activated.
\par 
\par \f2\fi-360 \b (22)\plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 \b  Choose Target Options\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  from the Targets menu to activate the Target Options menu.
\par 
\par The option Use Development Switches should appear in grey text with a check mark next to it.  Development Switches automatically include debugging information in the target.  If you were to select Use Release Switches, the IDE would select options useful for a production version of your program.  Selecting this option turns the text grey and places a check mark beside it.  The IDE uses the development switches by default.
\par 
\par \li720 \f2\fi-360 \b Note:\plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 For the purpose of this session, you should make the target with the development switches on.  If this is not the option selected, select it and remake the target before debugging.
\par \li360\fi0 
\par \f2\fi-360 \b (23)\plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 \b  Choose Debug\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  from the Targets menu.
\par 
\par The IDE starts the Open Watcom Debugger.
\par 
\par \f2\fi-360 \b (24)\plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 \b  Choose Step Over\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  from the Run menu of the Open Watcom Debugger to step through the code.  Choose\b  Go\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  from the Run menu of the Open Watcom Debugger to continue execution to the end of the code.  For more information on using the Open Watcom Debugger, refer to the\i \b  Open Watcom Debugger User's Guide.\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0 \b   The task has completed running\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  appears when the program has completed execution.
\par 
\par \f2\fi-360 \b (25)\plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 \b  Choose Exit\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  from the File menu to terminate the debugging session.
\par 
\par The Open Watcom Debugger closes and you return to the Open Watcom IDE Project window.  You can now exit the IDE.
\par 
\par \f2\fi-360 \b (26)\plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 \b  Choose Exit\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  from the File menu to exit the IDE.
\par 
\par The IDE prompts you to save the current project if you have not saved it since the last change.
\par \pard \page #{\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 # Review_of_What_Happened}${\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 $ Review of What Happened}!{\footnote ! ChangeButtonBinding( "btn_up", "JumpId( `ide.hlp', `Getting_Started_with_a_Short_Tutorial')") }K{\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 K ;Integrated Development Environment, tutorial review}+{\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 + Open Watcom Integrated Development Environment:0013}{\keepn \f2 \fs28 Review of What Happened \f2 \fs20 \par \pard \f2 \fs20 \tx360 \tx720 \tx1080 \tx1440 \tx1800 \tx2160 \tx2520 \tx2880 \tx3240 \tx3600 \tx3960 \tx4320 \tx4680 \tx5040 \tx5400 }
\par In this session, you created a project called\f4 \fs24  first.wpj.\f2 \fs20   This project had one target called\f4 \fs24  target1.exe,\f2 \fs20  a 32-bit Windows executable (unless you specified another target type).  Using the Open Watcom Editor, you entered and edited the source text in a file called\f4 \fs24  source1.c\f2 \fs20  or\f4 \fs24  source1.for\f2 \fs20  (if you tried the FORTRAN example).  You compiled and linked it into an executable program using the Open Watcom Make utility, a Open Watcom compiler, and the Open Watcom Linker.  You ran it directly and under the control of the Open Watcom Debugger.
\par 
\par When you saved the project, the Open Watcom Integrated Development Environment created the following permanent files:
\par 
\par \li360 \f2\fi-360 \b first.wpj\plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 Describes the screen layout and refers to a file called\f4 \fs24  target1.tgt\f2 \fs20 
\par 
\par \f2\fi-360 \b target1.tgt\plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 Describes the target executable\f4 \fs24  target1.exe\f2 \fs20  and all switches required to link it.  It also describes\f4 \fs24  source1.c\f2 \fs20  (or\f4 \fs24  source1.for\f2 \fs20 ) and the switches required to compile it.
\par \pard \page #{\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 # The_Open_Watcom_IDE_Project_Window}${\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 $ The Open Watcom IDE Project Window}!{\footnote ! ChangeButtonBinding( "btn_up", "JumpId( `ide.hlp', `Open_Watcom_Integrated_Development_Environment')") }K{\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 K ;project window, Integrated Development Environment;Integrated Development Environment, project window}+{\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 + Open Watcom Integrated Development Environment:0014}{\keepn \f2 \fs28 The Open Watcom IDE Project Window \f2 \fs20 \par \pard \f2 \fs20 \tx360 \tx720 \tx1080 \tx1440 \tx1800 \tx2160 \tx2520 \tx2880 \tx3240 \tx3600 \tx3960 \tx4320 \tx4680 \tx5040 \tx5400 }
\par When you first start the Open Watcom Integrated Development Environment, an empty Open Watcom IDE Project window opens.  This window represents the project that is currently open in the IDE.  The name of the current project appears in the title bar of this window.
\par 
\par The first thing you need to do is open a project for the Open Watcom IDE Project window.  This can be a new project you create or an existing project you open.
\par 
\par \li360 \f2\fi-360 \b Important:\plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 You can have only one project open at a time in the IDE.  If you attempt to open or create a project while one already exists in the IDE window, a message box appears informing you that the IDE is closing the current project and asks if you want to save any changes made to that project.  Choose Yes, No, or Cancel in this box to continue.
\par \pard \page #{\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 # Opening_a_Project}${\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 $ Opening a Project}!{\footnote ! ChangeButtonBinding( "btn_up", "JumpId( `ide.hlp', `The_Open_Watcom_IDE_Project_Window')") }K{\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 K ;open project;new project;opening a project;Integrated Development Environment, opening a project}+{\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 + Open Watcom Integrated Development Environment:0015}{\keepn \f2 \fs28 Opening a Project \f2 \fs20 \par \pard \f2 \fs20 \tx360 \tx720 \tx1080 \tx1440 \tx1800 \tx2160 \tx2520 \tx2880 \tx3240 \tx3600 \tx3960 \tx4320 \tx4680 \tx5040 \tx5400 }
\par \li360 \f2\fi-360 \b To create a new project:\plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 
\par 
\par \f2\fi-360 \b (1)\plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 \b  Choose New Project\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  from the File menu.
\par 
\par The Enter Project Filename dialog appears on the screen where you enter the name of the project you are creating.
\par 
\par \f2\fi-360 \b (2)\plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 \b  Enter the desired name\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  in the File Name field and choose the desired directory.
\par 
\par All projects must have the extension ".wpj".
\par 
\par \f4 \fs24 \qc \{bmc ide11.bmp\}\f2 \fs20 
\par \ql \i \b Figure 11.\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0 \i \b \b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0 \b  Use the Enter Project Filename dialog to create a new project.\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0 
\par 
\par \f2\fi-360 \b (3)\plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 \b  Click on OK\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  when completed.
\par 
\par The Enter Project Filename dialog closes and the new project name appears in the title bar of the Open Watcom IDE Project window.  A New Target dialog opens on the screen where you add a target to the new project.  Refer to the section Adding a Target for more information.
\par \li0\fi0 
\par \li360 \f2\fi-360 \b To open an existing project:\plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 
\par 
\par \f2\fi-360 \b (1)\plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 \b  Choose Open Project\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  from the File menu.
\par 
\par The Open dialog appears on the screen where you select the project you want to open.
\par 
\par \f4 \fs24 \qc \{bmc ide12.bmp\}\f2 \fs20 
\par \ql \i \b Figure 12.\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0 \i \b \b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0 \b  Use the Open dialog to open an existing project.\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0 
\par 
\par \f2\fi-360 \b (2)\plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 \b  Choose the desired project.\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0 
\par 
\par All projects have the extension ".wpj".
\par 
\par \f2\fi-360 \b (3)\plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 \b  Click on OK\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  when the desired project appears in the File Name field.
\par 
\par The Open dialog closes and the name of the selected project appears in the title bar of the Open Watcom IDE Project window.  If targets exist for the selected project, windows representing the targets appear in the main window.
\par \pard \page #{\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 # Closing_a_Project}${\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 $ Closing a Project}!{\footnote ! ChangeButtonBinding( "btn_up", "JumpId( `ide.hlp', `The_Open_Watcom_IDE_Project_Window')") }K{\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 K ;close project;closing a project;Integrated Development Environment, closing a project}+{\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 + Open Watcom Integrated Development Environment:0016}{\keepn \f2 \fs28 Closing a Project \f2 \fs20 \par \pard \f2 \fs20 \tx360 \tx720 \tx1080 \tx1440 \tx1800 \tx2160 \tx2520 \tx2880 \tx3240 \tx3600 \tx3960 \tx4320 \tx4680 \tx5040 \tx5400 }
\par \li360 \f2\fi-360 \b To close the current project:\plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 
\par \f2\fi-360 \b (1)\plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 \b  Choose Close Project\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  from the File menu.
\par 
\par The IDE removes all target windows for the current project from the Open Watcom IDE Project window.  The project name disappears from the title bar and the window is empty.
\par \pard \page #{\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 # Saving_a_Project}${\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 $ Saving a Project}!{\footnote ! ChangeButtonBinding( "btn_up", "JumpId( `ide.hlp', `The_Open_Watcom_IDE_Project_Window')") }K{\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 K ;saving a project;Integrated Development Environment, saving a project}+{\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 + Open Watcom Integrated Development Environment:0017}{\keepn \f2 \fs28 Saving a Project \f2 \fs20 \par \pard \f2 \fs20 \tx360 \tx720 \tx1080 \tx1440 \tx1800 \tx2160 \tx2520 \tx2880 \tx3240 \tx3600 \tx3960 \tx4320 \tx4680 \tx5040 \tx5400 }
\par There are two ways of saving your current project.  You can perform a simple save where the project is updated with any changes made since the last save or you can save the project under a different project name using the Save Project As function.
\par \pard \page #{\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 # Updating_the_Project}${\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 $ Updating the Project}!{\footnote ! ChangeButtonBinding( "btn_up", "JumpId( `ide.hlp', `Saving_a_Project')") }K{\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 K ;save project;updating a project;Integrated Development Environment, updating a project}+{\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 + Open Watcom Integrated Development Environment:0018}{\keepn \f2 \fs28 Updating the Project \f2 \fs20 \par \pard \f2 \fs20 \tx360 \tx720 \tx1080 \tx1440 \tx1800 \tx2160 \tx2520 \tx2880 \tx3240 \tx3600 \tx3960 \tx4320 \tx4680 \tx5040 \tx5400 }
\par \li360 \f2\fi-360 \b To perform a simple save on your project:\plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 
\par \f2\fi-360 \b (1)\plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 \b  Choose Save Project\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  from the File menu.
\par 
\par The IDE updates the project specified in the title bar.  The status bar along the bottom of the window indicates that the save function is being executed.
\par \pard \page #{\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 # Saving_the_Project_Under_a_New_Name}${\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 $ Saving the Project Under a New Name}!{\footnote ! ChangeButtonBinding( "btn_up", "JumpId( `ide.hlp', `Saving_a_Project')") }K{\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 K ;save project as;saving a project under a new name;Integrated Development Environment, saving a project under a new name}+{\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 + Open Watcom Integrated Development Environment:0019}{\keepn \f2 \fs28 Saving the Project Under a New Name \f2 \fs20 \par \pard \f2 \fs20 \tx360 \tx720 \tx1080 \tx1440 \tx1800 \tx2160 \tx2520 \tx2880 \tx3240 \tx3600 \tx3960 \tx4320 \tx4680 \tx5040 \tx5400 }
\par \li360 \f2\fi-360 \b To save the current project using a different name:\plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 
\par \f2\fi-360 \b (1)\plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 \b  Choose Save Project As\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  from the File menu.
\par 
\par The Save As dialog appears on the screen where you specify the file name by which you want to save the current project.
\par 
\par \f2\fi-360 \b (2)\plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 \b  Enter the desired project name\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  in the File Name field.
\par 
\par This is the new name by which you want to save the current project.  This can be the name of a new project or an existing project.
\par 
\par \li720 \f2\fi-360 \b Important:\plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 Entering the name of an existing project overwrites the contents of that project with the contents of the current project.
\par \li360\fi0 
\par \f2\fi-360 \b (3)\plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 \b  Choose the desired directory\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  for the project using the Directories list on the Save As dialog.
\par 
\par \f2\fi-360 \b (4)\plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 \b  Click on OK\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  when completed.
\par 
\par The Save As dialog closes and the new project name appears in the title bar of the Open Watcom IDE Project window.
\par \pard \page #{\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 # Working_with_Targets}${\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 $ Working with Targets}!{\footnote ! ChangeButtonBinding( "btn_up", "JumpId( `ide.hlp', `Open_Watcom_Integrated_Development_Environment')") }K{\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 K ;targets;Integrated Development Environment, targets}+{\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 + Open Watcom Integrated Development Environment:0020}{\keepn \f2 \fs28 Working with Targets \f2 \fs20 \par \pard \f2 \fs20 \tx360 \tx720 \tx1080 \tx1440 \tx1800 \tx2160 \tx2520 \tx2880 \tx3240 \tx3600 \tx3960 \tx4320 \tx4680 \tx5040 \tx5400 }
\par Once you have created a project, it is a simple matter to add additional targets.  You can also change the name of a target, remove targets, change the target environment, and change the image type.  This section discusses the following topics:
\par 
\par \li360 \fi-360\f1 \'b7\f2\tab Adding a Target
\par \fi0 \fi-360\f1 \'b7\f2\tab Minimizing a Target
\par \fi0 \fi-360\f1 \'b7\f2\tab Removing a Target
\par \fi0 \fi-360\f1 \'b7\f2\tab Renaming a Target
\par \fi0 \fi-360\f1 \'b7\f2\tab Marking a Target for Remake
\par \fi0 \fi-360\f1 \'b7\f2\tab Making Targets
\par \fi0 \fi-360\f1 \'b7\f2\tab Running a Target
\par \pard \page #{\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 # Adding_a_Target}${\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 $ Adding a Target}!{\footnote ! ChangeButtonBinding( "btn_up", "JumpId( `ide.hlp', `Working_with_Targets')") }K{\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 K ;targets, adding;Integrated Development Environment, adding a target}+{\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 + Open Watcom Integrated Development Environment:0021}{\keepn \f2 \fs28 Adding a Target \f2 \fs20 \par \pard \f2 \fs20 \tx360 \tx720 \tx1080 \tx1440 \tx1800 \tx2160 \tx2520 \tx2880 \tx3240 \tx3600 \tx3960 \tx4320 \tx4680 \tx5040 \tx5400 }
\par You add targets to the current project using the New Target dialog.  When you create a new project, this dialog appears automatically.  You have two options when adding targets to a project:  you can either enter the name of a new target or you can choose an existing target, previously created in another project.  This section describes both methods for adding a target.
\par 
\par \li360 \f2\fi-360 \b To add a new target to the current project:\plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 
\par \f2\fi-360 \b (1)\plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 \b  Choose New Target\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  from the Targets menu.
\par 
\par The New Target dialog appears on the screen where you enter the details of the target you are adding.
\par 
\par \f4 \fs24 \qc \{bmc ide13.bmp\}\f2 \fs20 
\par \ql \i \b Figure 13.\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0 \i \b \b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0 \b  Enter the new target details in the New Target dialog.\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0 
\par 
\par \f2\fi-360 \b (2)\plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 \b  Enter the target name\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  in the Target Name field.
\par 
\par Enter the entire pathname to ensure the directory is created where you want.  Depending on the nature of the target, it may or may not be able to share the same directory as other pre-existing targets.  Generally, if the target shares one or more of the same source files as other targets in the project, then it must be placed in its own subdirectory.  The object files that are produced for the target may have different attributes than a pre-existing target's object files (e.g., 16-bit vs 32-bit).  This is why the target should be placed in a separate subdirectory.
\par 
\par \f2\fi-360 \b (3)\plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 \b  Select the desired Target Environment and Image Type\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  as desired.
\par 
\par \f2\fi-360 \b (4)\plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 \b  Click on OK\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  when the Target Name, Target Environment and Image Type fields are correct.
\par 
\par If you specify a new directory in the target pathname, a message box appears asking if you want to create the specified directory.  Choose Yes or No to continue.  A window representing the new target appears on the Open Watcom IDE Project window.  The target name appears in the title bar.
\par 
\par The first line in this window describes the target.  It specifies the current mode, the target type.  The current mode is represented by either the letter D or R which appears in parentheses in front of the target type.  The letter D indicates that the target is placed in Development mode.  The letter R indicates that the target is placed in Release mode.  The rest of the window displays the source files for the target.
\par \li0\fi0 
\par \f4 \fs24 \qc \{bmc ide14.bmp\}\f2 \fs20 
\par \ql \i \b Figure 14.\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0 \i \b \b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0 \b  A project may have multiple targets.\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0 
\par 
\par \li360 \f2\fi-360 \b To add an existing target to a current project:\plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 
\par \f2\fi-360 \b (1)\plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 \b  Choose New Target\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  from the Targets menu.
\par 
\par The New Target dialog appears on the screen.
\par 
\par \f2\fi-360 \b (2)\plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 \b  Click on the Browse button.\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0 
\par 
\par The Enter Target Filename dialog appears on the screen.  This dialog allows you to browse the directories and files and select the ".tgt" file for the target you want to add.
\par 
\par \f4 \fs24 \qc \{bmc ide15.bmp\}\f2 \fs20 
\par \ql \i \b Figure 15.\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0 \i \b \b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0 \b  Choose an existing target from the Pick Target Filename dialog for the current project.\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0 
\par 
\par \f2\fi-360 \b (3)\plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 \b  Click on the desired target\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  to select it.
\par 
\par The selected target appears in the File Name field.
\par 
\par \f2\fi-360 \b (4)\plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 \b  Click on OK\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  to process the target selection.
\par 
\par The Enter Target Filename dialog closes.
\par 
\par \f2\fi-360 \b (5)\plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 \b  Click on OK\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  to close the New Target dialog.
\par 
\par A window representing the new target appears on the Open Watcom IDE Project window.  The target name appears in the title bar.
\par 
\par The first line in this window describes the target.  It specifies the current mode and the target type.  The current mode is represented by either the letter D or R which appears in parentheses in front of the target type.  The letter D indicates that the target is placed in development mode.  The letter R indicates that the target is placed in Release mode.  The rest of the window displays the source files for the target.
\par \pard \page #{\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 # Minimizing_a_Target}${\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 $ Minimizing a Target}!{\footnote ! ChangeButtonBinding( "btn_up", "JumpId( `ide.hlp', `Working_with_Targets')") }K{\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 K ;minimizing a target;Integrated Development Environment, minimizing a target}+{\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 + Open Watcom Integrated Development Environment:0022}{\keepn \f2 \fs28 Minimizing a Target \f2 \fs20 \par \pard \f2 \fs20 \tx360 \tx720 \tx1080 \tx1440 \tx1800 \tx2160 \tx2520 \tx2880 \tx3240 \tx3600 \tx3960 \tx4320 \tx4680 \tx5040 \tx5400 }
\par If you are working with a project that has multiple targets, the Open Watcom IDE Project window can become cluttered.  You can tidy your window by minimizing the target windows that you are not currently using.
\par 
\par \li360 \f2\fi-360 \b To minimize a target window:\plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 
\par \f2\fi-360 \b (1)\plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 \b  Click on the system box\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  in the upper left-hand corner of the target window you want to close.
\par 
\par A menu appears listing the functions you can perform on the selected window.
\par 
\par \f2\fi-360 \b (2)\plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 \b  Choose Minimize\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  from the menu.
\par 
\par The selected target window closes and an icon appears along the bottom of the screen representing that window.  The name of the target appears on or under the icon.
\par 
\par \f2\fi-360 \b (3)\plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 \b  Double-click on the icon\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  to open the target window again.
\par \li0\fi0 
\par \f4 \fs24 \qc \{bmc ide16.bmp\}\f2 \fs20 
\par \ql \i \b Figure 16.\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0 \i \b \b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0 \b  Minimize targets to reduce clutter in the project window.\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0 
\par \pard \page #{\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 # Removing_a_Target}${\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 $ Removing a Target}!{\footnote ! ChangeButtonBinding( "btn_up", "JumpId( `ide.hlp', `Working_with_Targets')") }K{\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 K ;removing a target;Integrated Development Environment, removing a target}+{\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 + Open Watcom Integrated Development Environment:0023}{\keepn \f2 \fs28 Removing a Target \f2 \fs20 \par \pard \f2 \fs20 \tx360 \tx720 \tx1080 \tx1440 \tx1800 \tx2160 \tx2520 \tx2880 \tx3240 \tx3600 \tx3960 \tx4320 \tx4680 \tx5040 \tx5400 }
\par The procedure for removing a target from a project is simple.  If your project has multiple targets, it is important to ensure that the target you want to remove is the one selected.  Removing a target does not permanently delete the target, it simply removes it from the current project.  If the target is attached to multiple projects, those projects will not be affected by the removal of the selected target from the current project.
\par 
\par \li360 \f2\fi-360 \b To remove a target from the current project:\plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 
\par \f2\fi-360 \b (1)\plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 \b  Click once on the target window\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  you want to remove from the project.
\par 
\par The title bar of the selected target window is highlighted.
\par 
\par \f2\fi-360 \b (2)\plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 \b  Choose Remove Target\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  from the Targets menu.
\par 
\par A message box appears on the screen asking if you really want to remove the selected target.
\par 
\par \f2\fi-360 \b (3)\plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 \b  Choose OK\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  to continue.
\par 
\par The message box closes and the selected target window disappears from the Open Watcom IDE Project window.
\par \pard \page #{\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 # Renaming_a_Target}${\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 $ Renaming a Target}!{\footnote ! ChangeButtonBinding( "btn_up", "JumpId( `ide.hlp', `Working_with_Targets')") }K{\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 K ;renaming a target;Integrated Development Environment, renaming a target}+{\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 + Open Watcom Integrated Development Environment:0024}{\keepn \f2 \fs28 Renaming a Target \f2 \fs20 \par \pard \f2 \fs20 \tx360 \tx720 \tx1080 \tx1440 \tx1800 \tx2160 \tx2520 \tx2880 \tx3240 \tx3600 \tx3960 \tx4320 \tx4680 \tx5040 \tx5400 }
\par When renaming a target, you can either give it a new name or you can give it the name of an existing target.  Choosing an existing target name overwrites the contents of the existing target with the contents of the current target.
\par 
\par \li360 \f2\fi-360 \b To rename a target:\plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 
\par \f2\fi-360 \b (1)\plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 \b  Click once on the target window\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  you want to rename.
\par 
\par The title bar of the selected target window is highlighted.
\par 
\par \f2\fi-360 \b (2)\plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 \b  Choose Rename Target\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  from the Targets menu.
\par 
\par The Rename Target dialog appears.  The Target Name, Target Environment, and Image Type of the selected target appear in the appropriate fields.
\par 
\par \f4 \fs24 \qc \{bmc ide17.bmp\}\f2 \fs20 
\par \ql \i \b Figure 17.\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0 \i \b \b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0 \b  Change a target's name using the Rename Target dialog.\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0 
\par 
\par \f2\fi-360 \b (3)\plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 \b  Enter the new target name\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  in the Target Name field.
\par 
\par or
\par 
\par \f2\fi-360 \b \plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 \b Click on the Browse button\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  and select an existing target name; click on OK when completed.
\par 
\par The selected or entered target name appears in the Target Name field.
\par 
\par \f2\fi-360 \b (4)\plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 \b  Verify that the Target Environment and Image Type are correct.\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0 
\par 
\par \f2\fi-360 \b (5)\plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 \b  Click on OK\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  on the Rename Target window.
\par 
\par The Rename Target dialog closes.  The title bar of the selected target window now displays the new target name.
\par \pard \page #{\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 # Marking_a_Target_for_Remake}${\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 $ Marking a Target for Remake}!{\footnote ! ChangeButtonBinding( "btn_up", "JumpId( `ide.hlp', `Working_with_Targets')") }K{\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 K ;marking a target;Integrated Development Environment, marking a target}+{\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 + Open Watcom Integrated Development Environment:0025}{\keepn \f2 \fs28 Marking a Target for Remake \f2 \fs20 \par \pard \f2 \fs20 \tx360 \tx720 \tx1080 \tx1440 \tx1800 \tx2160 \tx2520 \tx2880 \tx3240 \tx3600 \tx3960 \tx4320 \tx4680 \tx5040 \tx5400 }
\par Marking a target for remake ensures that the IDE will perform the link the next time you make that target or make all targets.  This function is necessary if you are not using the Autodepend function in the Setup Target submenu under the Targets menu.  By marking a target for remake, you control when and which targets need to be remade.
\par 
\par \li360 \f2\fi-360 \b To mark a target for remake:\plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 
\par \f2\fi-360 \b (1)\plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 \b  Click once on the target window\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  you want to mark.
\par 
\par The title bar of the selected target window is highlighted.
\par 
\par \f2\fi-360 \b (2)\plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 \b  Choose Mark Target for Remake\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  from the Targets menu.
\par 
\par A message box appears asking if you want to also mark all of the source files of the selected target.
\par 
\par \f2\fi-360 \b (3)\plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 \b  Choose Yes or No\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  to continue.
\par 
\par Choosing Yes marks all of the source files for the selected target as well as the target itself.  This means that the next time you do a make, the source files for the selected target will be compiled.  Choosing No marks only the selected target for remake.  In both cases, the message box closes and you return to the Open Watcom IDE Project window.
\par \pard \page #{\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 # Making_Targets}${\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 $ Making Targets}!{\footnote ! ChangeButtonBinding( "btn_up", "JumpId( `ide.hlp', `Working_with_Targets')") }K{\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 K ;making a target;Integrated Development Environment, making a target}+{\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 + Open Watcom Integrated Development Environment:0026}{\keepn \f2 \fs28 Making Targets \f2 \fs20 \par \pard \f2 \fs20 \tx360 \tx720 \tx1080 \tx1440 \tx1800 \tx2160 \tx2520 \tx2880 \tx3240 \tx3600 \tx3960 \tx4320 \tx4680 \tx5040 \tx5400 }
\par The Make function in the Targets menu creates an executable for the selected target.  When you perform this function, the IDE compiles the source files for the selected target into object files.  The object files are then linked together to create the target file.
\par 
\par There are two make functions available to you for targets:  Make and Make All.  The Make All function in the Actions menu tells the IDE to compile and link all of the targets for the current project.  This can be a lengthy process, depending upon the number of targets in your project, so you may choose to only make targets to which changes have been made.  This enables you to see if they can be made successfully.
\par 
\par Performing the Make function opens the IDE Log window which allows you to monitor the progress of the make.
\par 
\par \li360 \f2\fi-360 \b To make a single target:\plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 
\par \f2\fi-360 \b (1)\plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 \b  Click once on the target window\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  you want to make to select it.
\par 
\par The title bar of the selected target window is highlighted.
\par 
\par \f2\fi-360 \b (2)\plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 \b  Choose Make\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  from the Targets menu.
\par 
\par The IDE Log window opens along the bottom of the Open Watcom IDE Project window.  In the log window you can monitor the make procedure.\b   Execution Complete\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  appears in the IDE Log window when the make is completed on the selected target.  If the make is successful an executable file for the selected target can be found in the directory associated with the target window.
\par 
\par \f4 \fs24 \qc \{bmc ide18.bmp\}\f2 \fs20 
\par \ql \i \b Figure 18.\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0 \i \b \b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0 \b  Use the IDE Log window to monitor the make procedure.\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0 
\par 
\par \li0\fi0 
\par \li360 \f2\fi-360 \b To make all targets for the current project:\plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 
\par \f2\fi-360 \b (1)\plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 \b  Choose Make All\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  from the Actions Menu.
\par 
\par The IDE Log window opens along the bottom of the Open Watcom IDE Project window.  In the log window you can monitor the make procedure.\b   Execution Complete\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  appears in the IDE Log window when the make is completed on the targets.  If the make is successful an executable file for each target in the project can be found in the directory associated with the target window.
\par \pard \page #{\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 # Viewing_the_Make_Command_for_a_Target}${\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 $ Viewing the Make Command for a Target}!{\footnote ! ChangeButtonBinding( "btn_up", "JumpId( `ide.hlp', `Making_Targets')") }K{\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 K ;viewing make commands;Integrated Development Environment, viewing make commands}+{\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 + Open Watcom Integrated Development Environment:0027}{\keepn \f2 \fs28 Viewing the Make Command for a Target \f2 \fs20 \par \pard \f2 \fs20 \tx360 \tx720 \tx1080 \tx1440 \tx1800 \tx2160 \tx2520 \tx2880 \tx3240 \tx3600 \tx3960 \tx4320 \tx4680 \tx5040 \tx5400 }
\par With the IDE you can use the Show Make Command function to display the makefile commands for the selected target.  These are the commands that will be used to compile the selected target when you choose the function Make from the Targets menu or Make All from the Actions menu.
\par 
\par \li360 \f2\fi-360 \b To view the make commands for targets:\plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 
\par \f2\fi-360 \b (1)\plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 \b  Click once on the target window\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  whose makefile commands you want to view.
\par 
\par The title bar of the selected target window is highlighted.
\par 
\par \f2\fi-360 \b (2)\plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 \b  Choose Target Options\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  from the Targets menu.
\par 
\par A submenu appears on the screen from the Target Options menu.
\par 
\par \f2\fi-360 \b (3)\plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 \b  Choose Show Make Command\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  from the Target Options submenu.
\par 
\par The Target Command dialog appears on the screen.  This dialog displays the makefile commands for the selected target.
\par 
\par \f4 \fs24 \qc \{bmc ide19.bmp\}\f2 \fs20 
\par \ql \i \b Figure 19.\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0 \i \b \b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0 \b  The Target Command dialog displays the makefile commands for the selected target.\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0 
\par 
\par \f2\fi-360 \b (4)\plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 \b  Click on OK\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  to close the dialog.
\par \pard \page #{\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 # Running_a_Target}${\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 $ Running a Target}!{\footnote ! ChangeButtonBinding( "btn_up", "JumpId( `ide.hlp', `Working_with_Targets')") }K{\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 K ;running a target;Integrated Development Environment, running a target}+{\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 + Open Watcom Integrated Development Environment:0028}{\keepn \f2 \fs28 Running a Target \f2 \fs20 \par \pard \f2 \fs20 \tx360 \tx720 \tx1080 \tx1440 \tx1800 \tx2160 \tx2520 \tx2880 \tx3240 \tx3600 \tx3960 \tx4320 \tx4680 \tx5040 \tx5400 }
\par Once you have compiled and linked your target using the Make function, you can run the target to execute the program.
\par 
\par \li360 \f2\fi-360 \b To run a target:\plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 
\par \f2\fi-360 \b (1)\plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 \b  Click once on the target window\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  you want to run.
\par 
\par The title bar of the selected target window is highlighted.
\par 
\par \f2\fi-360 \b (2)\plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 \b  Choose Run\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  from the Targets menu.
\par 
\par The IDE executes the target program.
\par 
\par \f4 \fs24 \qc \{bmc ide20.bmp\}\f2 \fs20 
\par \ql \i \b Figure 20.\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0 \i \b \b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0 \b  Run from the Targets menu runs the target.\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0 
\par 
\par \pard \page #{\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 # Other_Run_Functions}${\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 $ Other Run Functions}!{\footnote ! ChangeButtonBinding( "btn_up", "JumpId( `ide.hlp', `Running_a_Target')") }K{\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 K ;running programs;Integrated Development Environment, running programs}+{\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 + Open Watcom Integrated Development Environment:0029}{\keepn \f2 \fs28 Other Run Functions \f2 \fs20 \par \pard \f2 \fs20 \tx360 \tx720 \tx1080 \tx1440 \tx1800 \tx2160 \tx2520 \tx2880 \tx3240 \tx3600 \tx3960 \tx4320 \tx4680 \tx5040 \tx5400 }
\par In the Open Watcom Integrated Development Environment you can run programs other than the targets in your current project window.  Use the run functions in the Actions menu to specify the program you want to run.  The Run item runs the specified program under your host environment.  The Run Batch item runs the specified program in the IDE Log window.
\par 
\par \li360 \f2\fi-360 \b To run other programs in the IDE:\plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 
\par \f2\fi-360 \b (1)\plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 \b  Choose Run\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  from the Actions menu.
\par 
\par or
\par 
\par \f2\fi-360 \b \plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 \b Choose Run Batch\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  from the Actions menu.
\par 
\par A Run or Run Batch dialog appears on the screen.  These dialogs are identical.
\par 
\par \f4 \fs24 \qc \{bmc ide21.bmp\}\f2 \fs20 
\par \ql \i \b Figure 21.\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0 \i \b \b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0 \b  Use the Run (or Run Batch) dialog to run other programs in the IDE.\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0 
\par 
\par \f2\fi-360 \b (2)\plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 \b  Enter the name of the program\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  you want to run in the Command field.
\par 
\par \f2\fi-360 \b (3)\plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 \b  Click on OK\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  to run the specified program.
\par 
\par The dialog closes and the specified program runs, either under the host environment or in the IDE Log window.
\par \pard \page #{\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 # Setting_Application_Parameters}${\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 $ Setting Application Parameters}!{\footnote ! ChangeButtonBinding( "btn_up", "JumpId( `ide.hlp', `Working_with_Targets')") }K{\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 K ;setting options;Integrated Development Environment, setting options}+{\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 + Open Watcom Integrated Development Environment:0030}{\keepn \f2 \fs28 Setting Application Parameters \f2 \fs20 \par \pard \f2 \fs20 \tx360 \tx720 \tx1080 \tx1440 \tx1800 \tx2160 \tx2520 \tx2880 \tx3240 \tx3600 \tx3960 \tx4320 \tx4680 \tx5040 \tx5400 }
\par The Targets and Sources menus and their submenus are divided into sections.  Horizontal lines separate each section.  The menu items in the bottom sections of the Targets and Sources menus are actions that you can perform on the selected target or source file in the current project.  If there are switches that can be set for these actions, there are corresponding menu item entries in the bottom sections of the submenus.  Choose Target Options and Source Options from their respective menus to display the submenus.
\par \pard \page #{\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 # Working_with_Source_Files}${\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 $ Working with Source Files}!{\footnote ! ChangeButtonBinding( "btn_up", "JumpId( `ide.hlp', `Open_Watcom_Integrated_Development_Environment')") }K{\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 K ;source files;Integrated Development Environment, source files}+{\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 + Open Watcom Integrated Development Environment:0031}{\keepn \f2 \fs28 Working with Source Files \f2 \fs20 \par \pard \f2 \fs20 \tx360 \tx720 \tx1080 \tx1440 \tx1800 \tx2160 \tx2520 \tx2880 \tx3240 \tx3600 \tx3960 \tx4320 \tx4680 \tx5040 \tx5400 }
\par Once your project has a target you need to add source files to the target.  The procedure for adding a source file to a target is simple.  Once you have source files that make up your target, you can also change the name of a source file and remove source files.  This section discusses the following topics:
\par 
\par \li360 \fi-360\f1 \'b7\f2\tab Adding Source Files
\par \fi0 \fi-360\f1 \'b7\f2\tab Removing Source Files
\par \fi0 \fi-360\f1 \'b7\f2\tab Renaming a Source File
\par \fi0 \fi-360\f1 \'b7\f2\tab Marking a Source File for Remake
\par \fi0 \fi-360\f1 \'b7\f2\tab Making Source Files
\par \fi0 \fi-360\f1 \'b7\f2\tab Viewing Included Files
\par \pard \page #{\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 # Adding_Source_Files}${\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 $ Adding Source Files}!{\footnote ! ChangeButtonBinding( "btn_up", "JumpId( `ide.hlp', `Working_with_Source_Files')") }K{\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 K ;extensions, .sqp;extensions, .sqc;extensions, .res;extensions, .rc;extensions, .hlp;extensions, .for;extensions, .cpp;extensions, .c;extensions, .asm;source files, adding;Integrated Development Environment, adding source files}+{\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 + Open Watcom Integrated Development Environment:0032}{\keepn \f2 \fs28 Adding Source Files \f2 \fs20 \par \pard \f2 \fs20 \tx360 \tx720 \tx1080 \tx1440 \tx1800 \tx2160 \tx2520 \tx2880 \tx3240 \tx3600 \tx3960 \tx4320 \tx4680 \tx5040 \tx5400 }
\par You add source files to the selected target (the target whose title bar is currently highlighted) using the Add File(s) dialog.  The current project must have a target in order to use the New Source function.  You have two options when adding source files to a target:  you can either enter the name of a new source file or you can choose an existing source file.  When adding existing source files you can use the file browser, type sources one at a time into the Add File(s) dialog, or use wild cards in the Add File(s) dialog.  This section describes all methods for adding source files.
\par 
\par The extension you use with the source file name determines the compiler that the IDE uses to create the target.  For example, the ".c" extension, invokes the Open Watcom C compiler to make the target.  You can specify several different compilers:
\par 
\par \li360 \f2\fi-360 \b xxx.asm\plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 The ".asm" extension invokes the Open Watcom Assembler.
\par 
\par \f2\fi-360 \b xxx.c\plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 The ".c" extension invokes the Open Watcom C compiler.
\par 
\par \f2\fi-360 \b xxx.cpp\plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 The ".cpp" extension invokes the Open Watcom C++ compiler.
\par 
\par \f2\fi-360 \b xxx.for\plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 The ".for" extension invokes the Open Watcom FORTRAN 77 compiler.
\par 
\par \f2\fi-360 \b xxx.hlp\plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 The ".hlp" extension invokes either the Win16, OS/2, or Win32 compiler.
\par 
\par \f2\fi-360 \b xxx.rc\plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 The ".rc" extension invokes the Open Watcom resource compiler.
\par 
\par \f2\fi-360 \b xxx.res\plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 The ".res" extension invokes the Open Watcom Resource Editor.
\par 
\par \f2\fi-360 \b xxx.sqc\plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 The ".sqc" extension invokes the embedded SQL preprocessor and the Open Watcom C compiler.
\par 
\par \f2\fi-360 \b xxx.sqp\plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 The ".sqp" extension invokes the embedded SQL preprocessor and the Open Watcom C++ compiler.
\par \li0\fi0 
\par \li360 \f2\fi-360 \b To add new source files to the selected target:\plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 
\par \f2\fi-360 \b (1)\plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 \b  Choose New Source\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  from the Sources menu.
\par 
\par The Add File(s) dialog appears where you specify the name(s) of the source file(s) you are adding.  Source names must be filenames with extensions; pathnames are not required.
\par 
\par For the OS/2 IDE, you can add multiple source files to the target at one time by inserting a space between each filename you enter into the filenames field.
\par 
\par For all other systems, you can enter the names of the source files (with extensions) in the File Name field, one at a time.  Once you have typed a name in the File Name field, you can add it to the list of source files to be included by clicking the Add button.  Individual names can be removed from the "add" list by selecting them and clicking the Remove button.
\par 
\par \f4 \fs24 \qc \{bmc ide22.bmp\}\f2 \fs20 
\par \ql \i \b Figure 22.\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0 \i \b \b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0 \b  Add one or several source files to a target using the Add File(s) dialog.\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0 
\par 
\par \f2\fi-360 \b (2)\plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 \b  Enter the source file name(s).\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0 
\par 
\par For the OS/2 IDE, enter all the names into the field separating each name by a space.  Click on OK when done.
\par 
\par For all other systems, enter one name at a time into the File Name field, pressing Enter or clicking on Add after each one has been entered.  Click on Close when done.
\par 
\par The Add File(s) dialog closes and the selected source file(s) appear in the selected target window on the Open Watcom IDE Project window.
\par 
\par The source files are grouped by their file extensions and appear in alphabetical order.  You can expand and collapse the list of source files by clicking on the folder icon to the left of the header.
\par \li0\fi0 
\par \li360 \f2\fi-360 \b To add existing source files to the selected target using the file browser:\plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 
\par \f2\fi-360 \b (1)\plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 \b  Choose New Source\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  from the Sources menu.
\par 
\par The Add File(s) dialog appears on the screen.
\par 
\par For the OS/2 IDE, click on the Browse button and the Pick Filename dialog appears on the screen.  This dialog allows you to browse the directories and files and select the source file(s) you want to add to the current target.  Click on the desired source file to select it.  The selected source file(s) appear highlighted in the Pick Filename dialog.  When done browsing files, click on OPEN to add the selected files.  The Pick Filename dialog closes.  When done selecting files, click on OK.
\par 
\par For all other systems, you can use the file browser to select files.  The type of files displayed in file list window can be controlled by modifying the List Files of Type field.  Once you have selected a name from the file list, you can add it to the list of source files to be included by clicking the Add button.  All files in the file list can be added by clicking the Add All button.  Individual names can be removed from the "add" list by selecting them and clicking the Remove button.  When done, click on Close to add the selected files.
\par 
\par The Add File(s) dialog closes and the selected source file(s) appear in the selected target window on the Open Watcom IDE Project window.
\par 
\par The source files are grouped by their file extensions and appear in alphabetical order.  You can expand and collapse the list of source files by clicking on the folder icon to the left of the header.
\par \li0\fi0 
\par \li360 \f2\fi-360 \b To add existing source files to the selected target using wild cards:\plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 
\par \f2\fi-360 \b (1)\plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 \b  Choose New Source\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  from the Sources menu.
\par 
\par The Add File(s) dialog appears on the screen.
\par 
\par For the OS/2 IDE, enter the source file name pattern in the filename(s) field using wild cards.  For example, enter\f4 \fs24  sou*.c\f2 \fs20  to add all source files whose name begins "sou" and have a ".c" extension to the current target.  When done, click on OK to add the selected files.
\par 
\par \i \b Hint:\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0   Even when entering wild cards, you can add multiple source file groups by separating each entry with one space (e.g., sou*.c sou*.cpp).
\par 
\par For all other systems, enter the source file name pattern in the File Name field using wild cards.  For example, enter\f4 \fs24  sou*.c\f2 \fs20  to select all source files whose name begins "sou" and have a ".c" extension.  Click on the Add All button to add all the names that match the wild card pattern.  Individual names can be removed from the "add" list by selecting them and clicking the Remove button.  When done, click on Close to add the selected files.
\par 
\par The Add File(s) dialog closes and the selected source file(s) appear in the selected target window on the Open Watcom IDE Project window.
\par 
\par The source files are grouped by their file extensions and appear in alphabetical order.  You can expand and collapse the list of source files by clicking on the folder icon to the left of the header.
\par \pard \page #{\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 # Removing_a_Source_File}${\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 $ Removing a Source File}!{\footnote ! ChangeButtonBinding( "btn_up", "JumpId( `ide.hlp', `Working_with_Source_Files')") }K{\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 K ;source files, removing;Integrated Development Environment, removing source files}+{\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 + Open Watcom Integrated Development Environment:0033}{\keepn \f2 \fs28 Removing a Source File \f2 \fs20 \par \pard \f2 \fs20 \tx360 \tx720 \tx1080 \tx1440 \tx1800 \tx2160 \tx2520 \tx2880 \tx3240 \tx3600 \tx3960 \tx4320 \tx4680 \tx5040 \tx5400 }
\par The procedure for removing a source file from a project is simple.  You can remove one source at a time or you can remove all source files with a common file extension from the target.  Removing a source file does not permanently delete the it.  The IDE simply removes it from the current target.  If the removed source file is common to multiple targets, the other targets will not be affected by the removal of the selected source file.
\par 
\par \li360 \f2\fi-360 \b To remove one source file from a target:\plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 
\par \f2\fi-360 \b (1)\plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 \b  Click once on the source file\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  you want to remove from the target.
\par 
\par The selected source file appears highlighted in the target window.
\par 
\par \f2\fi-360 \b (2)\plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 \b  Choose Remove Source\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  from the Sources menu.
\par 
\par A message box appears asking if you are sure you want to remove the selected source file.
\par 
\par \f2\fi-360 \b (3)\plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 \b  Click on the Yes button.\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0 
\par 
\par The message box closes and the IDE removes the selected source file from the target window.
\par \li0\fi0 
\par \li360 \f2\fi-360 \b To remove a group of source files from a target:\plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 
\par \f2\fi-360 \b (1)\plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 \b  Click once on the header\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  to the right of the folder icon of the source files group you want to remove from the target.
\par 
\par The selected header appears highlighted in the target window.
\par 
\par \f4 \fs24 \qc \{bmc ide23.bmp\}\f2 \fs20 
\par \ql \i \b Figure 23.\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0 \i \b \b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0 \b  Click on the header to remove all source files with a common file extension.\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0 
\par 
\par \f2\fi-360 \b (2)\plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 \b  Choose Remove Source\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  from the Sources menu.
\par 
\par A message window appears asking if you want to remove all source files with the selected file extension.
\par 
\par \f2\fi-360 \b (3)\plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 \b  Choose OK\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  to continue.
\par 
\par The message box closes and the IDE removes the selected group of source files from the target.
\par \pard \page #{\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 # Renaming_a_Source_File}${\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 $ Renaming a Source File}!{\footnote ! ChangeButtonBinding( "btn_up", "JumpId( `ide.hlp', `Working_with_Source_Files')") }K{\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 K ;source files, renaming;Integrated Development Environment, renaming source files}+{\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 + Open Watcom Integrated Development Environment:0034}{\keepn \f2 \fs28 Renaming a Source File \f2 \fs20 \par \pard \f2 \fs20 \tx360 \tx720 \tx1080 \tx1440 \tx1800 \tx2160 \tx2520 \tx2880 \tx3240 \tx3600 \tx3960 \tx4320 \tx4680 \tx5040 \tx5400 }
\par When renaming a source file, you can either give it a new name or you can give it the name of an existing source file.  Choosing an existing source file name overwrites the contents of that source file with the contents of the current source file.
\par 
\par \li360 \f2\fi-360 \b To rename a source file:\plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 
\par \f2\fi-360 \b (1)\plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 \b  Click once on the source file\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  you want to rename.
\par 
\par The selected source file appears highlighted in the target window.
\par 
\par \f2\fi-360 \b (2)\plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 \b  Choose Rename Source\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  from the Sources menu.
\par 
\par The Rename File dialog appears where you enter the new name of the selected source file.
\par 
\par \f4 \fs24 \qc \{bmc ide24.bmp\}\f2 \fs20 
\par \ql \i \b Figure 24.\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0 \i \b \b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0 \b  Use the Rename File dialog to rename the selected source file.\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0 
\par 
\par \f2\fi-360 \b (3)\plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 \b  Enter the new source file name\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  in the field.
\par 
\par or
\par 
\par \f2\fi-360 \b \plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 \b Click on the Browse button\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  and select an existing source file name; click on OK when completed.
\par 
\par The selected or entered source file name appears in the Rename File dialog.
\par 
\par \f2\fi-360 \b (4)\plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 \b  Click on OK\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  on the Rename File dialog.
\par 
\par The Rename File dialog closes.  The target window displays the updated source file.
\par \pard \page #{\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 # Marking_a_Source_File_for_Remake}${\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 $ Marking a Source File for Remake}!{\footnote ! ChangeButtonBinding( "btn_up", "JumpId( `ide.hlp', `Working_with_Source_Files')") }K{\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 K ;source files, marking for remake;Integrated Development Environment, marking source files for remake}+{\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 + Open Watcom Integrated Development Environment:0035}{\keepn \f2 \fs28 Marking a Source File for Remake \f2 \fs20 \par \pard \f2 \fs20 \tx360 \tx720 \tx1080 \tx1440 \tx1800 \tx2160 \tx2520 \tx2880 \tx3240 \tx3600 \tx3960 \tx4320 \tx4680 \tx5040 \tx5400 }
\par The Mark Source for Remake function in the Sources menu enables you to mark a source file to ensure that the IDE will recompile it the next time you make the target.  This function is useful because although changes may not have been made to the selected source file, changes may have been made to a source file on which the selected source file depends.  This function is necessary if you are not using the Autodepend function in the Target Options submenu under the Targets menu.  Marking source files for remake gives you control over when and which source files need to be remade.
\par 
\par With the Mark function you can mark an individual source file or a group of source files with a common file extension.
\par 
\par \li360 \f2\fi-360 \b To mark a source file or a group of source files for remake:\plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 
\par \f2\fi-360 \b (1)\plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 \b  Click once on the source file\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  you want to mark.
\par 
\par or
\par 
\par \f2\fi-360 \b \plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 \b Click once on the header\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  of the group of source files you want to mark.
\par 
\par The selection is highlighted in the target window.
\par 
\par \f2\fi-360 \b (2)\plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 \b  Choose Mark Source for Remake\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  from the Sources menu.
\par 
\par If you selected a group of source files to mark, a message box appears asking if you want to also mark all of the source files of the specified extension.
\par 
\par \f2\fi-360 \b (3)\plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 \b  Choose Yes\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  to continue.
\par 
\par The message box disappears and the IDE marks all of the source files in the selected group to be recompiled.
\par \pard \page #{\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 # Making_Source_Files}${\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 $ Making Source Files}!{\footnote ! ChangeButtonBinding( "btn_up", "JumpId( `ide.hlp', `Working_with_Source_Files')") }K{\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 K ;source files, making;Integrated Development Environment, making source files}+{\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 + Open Watcom Integrated Development Environment:0036}{\keepn \f2 \fs28 Making Source Files \f2 \fs20 \par \pard \f2 \fs20 \tx360 \tx720 \tx1080 \tx1440 \tx1800 \tx2160 \tx2520 \tx2880 \tx3240 \tx3600 \tx3960 \tx4320 \tx4680 \tx5040 \tx5400 }
\par The Make function in the Sources menu enables you to compile a selected source file into an object file.  You can also perform the Make function on a group of source files with a common file extension.
\par 
\par Performing the Make function opens the IDE Log window which allows you to monitor the progress of the make.
\par 
\par \li360 \f2\fi-360 \b To make source files:\plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 
\par \f2\fi-360 \b (1)\plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 \b  Click once on the source file\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  you want to make.
\par 
\par or
\par 
\par \f2\fi-360 \b \plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 \b Click once on the header\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  of the group of source files you want to make.
\par 
\par The selected source files are highlighted in the target window.
\par 
\par \f2\fi-360 \b (2)\plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 \b  Choose Make\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  from the Sources menu.
\par 
\par The IDE Log window opens along the bottom of the Open Watcom IDE Project window.  In the log window you can monitor the make procedure.\b   Execution Complete\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  appears in the IDE Log window when the make is completed on the selected source files.
\par \li0\fi0 
\par \li360 \f2\fi-360 \b To make all targets for the current project:\plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 
\par \f2\fi-360 \b (1)\plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 \b  Choose Make All\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  from the Actions Menu.
\par 
\par The IDE Log window opens along the bottom of the Open Watcom IDE Project window.  In the log window you can monitor the make procedure.\b   Execution Complete\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  appears in the IDE Log window when the make is completed on the targets.  If the make is successful an executable file for each target in the project can be found in the directory associated with the target window.
\par \pard \page #{\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 # Viewing_the_Make_Command_for_a_Source_File}${\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 $ Viewing the Make Command for a Source File}!{\footnote ! ChangeButtonBinding( "btn_up", "JumpId( `ide.hlp', `Making_Source_Files')") }K{\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 K ;viewing make commands;Integrated Development Environment, viewing make commands}+{\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 + Open Watcom Integrated Development Environment:0037}{\keepn \f2 \fs28 Viewing the Make Command for a Source File \f2 \fs20 \par \pard \f2 \fs20 \tx360 \tx720 \tx1080 \tx1440 \tx1800 \tx2160 \tx2520 \tx2880 \tx3240 \tx3600 \tx3960 \tx4320 \tx4680 \tx5040 \tx5400 }
\par With the IDE you can use the Show Make Command function to display the makefile commands for the selected source file.  These are the commands that will be used to compile the selected source file when you choose the function Make from the Sources menu.
\par 
\par \li360 \f2\fi-360 \b To view the make commands for source files:\plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 
\par \f2\fi-360 \b (1)\plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 \b  Click once on the source file\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  whose makefile commands you want to view.
\par 
\par or
\par 
\par \f2\fi-360 \b \plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 \b Click once on the header\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  of the group of source files whose makefile commands you want to view.
\par 
\par The selected source file or group header is highlighted.
\par 
\par \f2\fi-360 \b (2)\plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 \b  Choose Source Options\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  from the Sources menu.
\par 
\par A submenu appears on the screen from the Source Options menu.
\par 
\par \f2\fi-360 \b (3)\plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 \b  Choose Show Make Command\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  from the Source Options submenu.
\par 
\par The Source Command dialog appears on the screen.  This dialog displays the makefile commands for the selected source file or source file header.
\par 
\par \f4 \fs24 \qc \{bmc ide25.bmp\}\f2 \fs20 
\par \ql \i \b Figure 25.\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0 \i \b \b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0 \b  The IDE Source Command dialog displays the makefile commands for the selected source file(s).\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0 
\par 
\par \f2\fi-360 \b (4)\plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 \b  Click on OK\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  to close the dialog.
\par \pard \page #{\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 # Viewing_Included_Files}${\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 $ Viewing Included Files}!{\footnote ! ChangeButtonBinding( "btn_up", "JumpId( `ide.hlp', `Working_with_Source_Files')") }K{\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 K ;viewing included files list;Integrated Development Environment, viewing included files list}+{\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 + Open Watcom Integrated Development Environment:0038}{\keepn \f2 \fs28 Viewing Included Files \f2 \fs20 \par \pard \f2 \fs20 \tx360 \tx720 \tx1080 \tx1440 \tx1800 \tx2160 \tx2520 \tx2880 \tx3240 \tx3600 \tx3960 \tx4320 \tx4680 \tx5040 \tx5400 }
\par The Included Files function in the Sources menu enables you to select a source file and view a list of all of the files included by that source file.  This is a list of all files on which the selected source file depends.  From this list you can select included files and edit them.
\par 
\par Because the selected source file depends on the files in this list, it is necessary to remake it if changes are made to any of the included files.  If you are using the Autodepend function, the IDE automatically remakes the source file if any of the included files are modified.
\par 
\par \li360 \f2\fi-360 \b To view the list of included files:\plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 
\par \f2\fi-360 \b (1)\plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 \b  Click on the source file\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  whose list of included files you want to view.
\par 
\par The selected source file is highlighted in the target window.
\par 
\par \f2\fi-360 \b (2)\plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 \b  Choose Included Files\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  from the Sources menu.
\par 
\par The Files Included dialog appears on the screen.  This dialog lists all source files on which the selected source files depends.
\par \li0\fi0 
\par \li360 \f2\fi-360 \b To edit a source file from the File Included dialog:\plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 
\par \f2\fi-360 \b (1)\plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 \b  Click once on the source file\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  in the included files list that you want to edit.
\par 
\par The selected source file is highlighted in the File Included dialog.
\par 
\par \f2\fi-360 \b (2)\plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 \b  Click on OK.\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0 
\par 
\par or
\par 
\par \f2\fi-360 \b \plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 \b Double-click\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  on the selected source file.
\par 
\par The Files Included dialog closes and the IDE invokes the appropriate editor for the selected included file.  The editor appears and you can edit the file.  Close the editor when you are finished making the desired changes to return to the Open Watcom IDE Project window.
\par \pard \page #{\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 # Using_the_IDE_Log_Window}${\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 $ Using the IDE Log Window}!{\footnote ! ChangeButtonBinding( "btn_up", "JumpId( `ide.hlp', `Open_Watcom_Integrated_Development_Environment')") }K{\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 K ;log window, using the;using the log window;Integrated Development Environment, using the log window}+{\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 + Open Watcom Integrated Development Environment:0039}{\keepn \f2 \fs28 Using the IDE Log Window \f2 \fs20 \par \pard \f2 \fs20 \tx360 \tx720 \tx1080 \tx1440 \tx1800 \tx2160 \tx2520 \tx2880 \tx3240 \tx3600 \tx3960 \tx4320 \tx4680 \tx5040 \tx5400 }
\par When you compile a target or source file, the IDE Log window appears along the bottom of the screen.  Once this window is open it remains on your screen until you close the project.  However, to maximize your project window space you can minimize the IDE Log window when it is not being used.
\par 
\par The Log menu contains three functions that you use in relation to the IDE Log window.  This section discusses the following functions:
\par 
\par \li360 \fi-360\f1 \'b7\f2\tab Stopping the Make Function
\par \fi0 \fi-360\f1 \'b7\f2\tab Getting Help
\par \fi0 \fi-360\f1 \'b7\f2\tab Editing the Source File
\par \fi0 \fi-360\f1 \'b7\f2\tab Saving the Log
\par \pard \page #{\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 # Stopping_the_Make_Function}${\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 $ Stopping the Make Function}!{\footnote ! ChangeButtonBinding( "btn_up", "JumpId( `ide.hlp', `Using_the_IDE_Log_Window')") }K{\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 K ;stopping the make;Integrated Development Environment, stopping the make}+{\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 + Open Watcom Integrated Development Environment:0040}{\keepn \f2 \fs28 Stopping the Make Function \f2 \fs20 \par \pard \f2 \fs20 \tx360 \tx720 \tx1080 \tx1440 \tx1800 \tx2160 \tx2520 \tx2880 \tx3240 \tx3600 \tx3960 \tx4320 \tx4680 \tx5040 \tx5400 }
\par Choosing to make a source file, target, or all targets can be a lengthy process.  The Stop function in the Log menu gives you the opportunity to cancel the make function once it has started.  This is a useful function in case you decide that you do not want to continue with the make.
\par 
\par \li360 \f2\fi-360 \b To stop the make function:\plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 
\par \f2\fi-360 \b (1)\plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 \b  Choose Stop\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  from the Log menu.
\par 
\par A message box appears asking if it is okay to interrupt the executing process.
\par 
\par \f2\fi-360 \b (2)\plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 \b  Click on OK\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  to continue.
\par 
\par The message box closes and the IDE stops the make function.  The IDE Log window displays the message\b  Stop Invoked\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  followed by the message\b  Execution Complete.\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0 
\par \pard \page #{\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 # Getting_Help}${\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 $ Getting Help}!{\footnote ! ChangeButtonBinding( "btn_up", "JumpId( `ide.hlp', `Using_the_IDE_Log_Window')") }K{\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 K ;getting help;Integrated Development Environment, getting help}+{\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 + Open Watcom Integrated Development Environment:0041}{\keepn \f2 \fs28 Getting Help \f2 \fs20 \par \pard \f2 \fs20 \tx360 \tx720 \tx1080 \tx1440 \tx1800 \tx2160 \tx2520 \tx2880 \tx3240 \tx3600 \tx3960 \tx4320 \tx4680 \tx5040 \tx5400 }
\par The Help on Message function in the Log menu gives you access to on-line help information for the selected error message in the IDE Log window.
\par 
\par \li360 \f2\fi-360 \b To reveal help for an error message:\plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 
\par \f2\fi-360 \b (1)\plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 \b  Click once on the error message\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  in the IDE Log window on which you want information.
\par 
\par The selected error message is highlighted in the IDE Log window.
\par 
\par \f2\fi-360 \b (2)\plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 \b  Choose Help on Message\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  from the Log menu.
\par 
\par This opens the Help window.  Information on the selected error message appears in this window.
\par 
\par \f2\fi-360 \b (3)\plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 \b  Choose Close\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  from the system menu on the Help window to cancel the Help facility.
\par 
\par The Help window closes and you return to the IDE Log window.
\par \pard \page #{\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 # Editing_the_Source_File}${\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 $ Editing the Source File}!{\footnote ! ChangeButtonBinding( "btn_up", "JumpId( `ide.hlp', `Using_the_IDE_Log_Window')") }K{\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 K ;source files, editing;editing source;Integrated Development Environment, editing source}+{\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 + Open Watcom Integrated Development Environment:0042}{\keepn \f2 \fs28 Editing the Source File \f2 \fs20 \par \pard \f2 \fs20 \tx360 \tx720 \tx1080 \tx1440 \tx1800 \tx2160 \tx2520 \tx2880 \tx3240 \tx3600 \tx3960 \tx4320 \tx4680 \tx5040 \tx5400 }
\par The Edit File function in the Log menu allows you to select an error message in the IDE Log window and edit the corresponding source file.  This function invokes the appropriate editor for the source file and positions your cursor in the source code where the error occurred (if the error message contained line and column information).
\par 
\par \li360 \f2\fi-360 \b To edit a source file:\plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 
\par \f2\fi-360 \b (1)\plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 \b  Click once on the error message\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  in the IDE Log window that you want to edit.
\par 
\par The selected error message is highlighted in the IDE Log window.
\par 
\par \f2\fi-360 \b (2)\plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 \b  Choose Edit File\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  from the Log menu.
\par 
\par or
\par 
\par \f2\fi-360 \b \plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 \b Double-click\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  on the selected error message.
\par 
\par Both of these actions open the appropriate editor.  If the Open Watcom Editor appears and the error message contained column and line details, your cursor will be positioned at the error in the source code.
\par 
\par \f2\fi-360 \b (3)\plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 \b  Make the required changes\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  to the source file and exit the Editor.
\par \pard \page #{\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 # Saving_the_Log}${\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 $ Saving the Log}!{\footnote ! ChangeButtonBinding( "btn_up", "JumpId( `ide.hlp', `Using_the_IDE_Log_Window')") }K{\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 K ;saving the log;Integrated Development Environment, saving the log}+{\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 + Open Watcom Integrated Development Environment:0043}{\keepn \f2 \fs28 Saving the Log \f2 \fs20 \par \pard \f2 \fs20 \tx360 \tx720 \tx1080 \tx1440 \tx1800 \tx2160 \tx2520 \tx2880 \tx3240 \tx3600 \tx3960 \tx4320 \tx4680 \tx5040 \tx5400 }
\par The Save Log As function in the Log menu allows you to save the contents of the Log window into a file.
\par 
\par \li360 \f2\fi-360 \b To save the log:\plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 
\par \f2\fi-360 \b (1)\plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 \b  Choose Save Log As\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  from the Log menu.
\par 
\par The Save Log As dialog appears where you specify the filename by which you want to save the contents of the Log window.
\par 
\par \f2\fi-360 \b (2)\plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 \b  Enter the desired log name\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  in the File Name field.
\par 
\par \f2\fi-360 \b (3)\plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 \b  Specify the directory\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  to which you want to save the log.
\par 
\par \f2\fi-360 \b (4)\plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 \b  Click on the OK button.\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0 
\par 
\par The IDE closes the Save Log As dialog and saves the current log as specified
\par \pard \page #{\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 # The_IDE_Switches}${\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 $ The IDE Switches}!{\footnote ! ChangeButtonBinding( "btn_up", "JumpId( `ide.hlp', `Open_Watcom_Integrated_Development_Environment')") }K{\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 K ;switches, setting;setting switches;Integrated Development Environment, setting switches}+{\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 + Open Watcom Integrated Development Environment:0044}{\keepn \f2 \fs28 The IDE Switches \f2 \fs20 \par \pard \f2 \fs20 \tx360 \tx720 \tx1080 \tx1440 \tx1800 \tx2160 \tx2520 \tx2880 \tx3240 \tx3600 \tx3960 \tx4320 \tx4680 \tx5040 \tx5400 }
\par There are a number of different switches you can set in the Open Watcom Integrated Development Environment.  Switches enables you to change the way the various tools, such as compilers and linkers, perform their jobs.  The switches discussed in this section are:
\par 
\par \li360 \fi-360\f1 \'b7\f2\tab Release and Development Switches
\par \fi0 \fi-360\f1 \'b7\f2\tab Target and Source File Switches
\par \fi0 \fi-360\f1 \'b7\f2\tab The Autodepend Switch
\par \fi0 \li0\fi0 
\par This section discusses the different switches available in the IDE and how you can use them.
\par \pard \page #{\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 # Using_Release_and_Development_Switches}${\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 $ Using Release and Development Switches}!{\footnote ! ChangeButtonBinding( "btn_up", "JumpId( `ide.hlp', `The_IDE_Switches')") }K{\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 K ;switches, development;development switches;Integrated Development Environment, development switches;switches, release;release switches;Integrated Development Environment, release switches}+{\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 + Open Watcom Integrated Development Environment:0045}{\keepn \f2 \fs28 Using Release and Development Switches \f2 \fs20 \par \pard \f2 \fs20 \tx360 \tx720 \tx1080 \tx1440 \tx1800 \tx2160 \tx2520 \tx2880 \tx3240 \tx3600 \tx3960 \tx4320 \tx4680 \tx5040 \tx5400 }
\par There are two sets of switches the IDE can use during the make process:  Development and Release switches.  You are able to specify different switches in each set for each target in your project.  The letters D and R in the target window to the left of the target type indicate which set of switches is being used.  You choose the switches you want the IDE to use from the Target Options submenu under the Targets menu.  The selected set of switches has a check mark beside it and appears in grey text.
\par 
\par \li360 \f2\fi-360 \b To change the selected set of switches for a target:\plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 
\par \f2\fi-360 \b (1)\plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 \b  Click once on the target\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  whose switches you want to change.
\par 
\par The title bar of the selected target is highlighted.
\par 
\par \f2\fi-360 \b (2)\plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 \b  Choose Target Options\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  from the Targets menu.
\par 
\par A submenu appears from the Target Options menu.
\par 
\par \f2\fi-360 \b (3)\plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 \b  Choose Use Development Switches or Use Release Switches\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  (whichever is not currently selected) from the submenu, as desired.
\par 
\par A message box appears asking if you want to mark the selected target and its source files for remake.
\par 
\par \f2\fi-360 \b (4)\plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 \b  Choose Yes or No,\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  as desired, to continue.
\par 
\par The message box closes and you return to the Open Watcom IDE window.  The next time you perform a make, the IDE will use the selected set of switches.
\par \pard \page #{\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 # Setting_Target_and_Source_File_Switches}${\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 $ Setting Target and Source File Switches}!{\footnote ! ChangeButtonBinding( "btn_up", "JumpId( `ide.hlp', `The_IDE_Switches')") }K{\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 K ;switches, setting;Integrated Development Environment, setting switches}+{\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 + Open Watcom Integrated Development Environment:0046}{\keepn \f2 \fs28 Setting Target and Source File Switches \f2 \fs20 \par \pard \f2 \fs20 \tx360 \tx720 \tx1080 \tx1440 \tx1800 \tx2160 \tx2520 \tx2880 \tx3240 \tx3600 \tx3960 \tx4320 \tx4680 \tx5040 \tx5400 }
\par You can set a variety of command-line compiler and linker options in the Open Watcom Integrated Development Environment.  This section shows you how to use the Switches dialogs to choose your options.
\par 
\par You can set the Integrated Development Environment to work in one of two switch modes:  Use Development Switches or Use Release Switches.  Either of these two options uses a default set of switch settings, unless you specify otherwise.  You can set switches for an individual target, specific source file type, or individual source file.
\par \pard \page #{\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 # Setting_Target_Switches}${\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 $ Setting Target Switches}!{\footnote ! ChangeButtonBinding( "btn_up", "JumpId( `ide.hlp', `Setting_Target_and_Source_File_Switches')") }K{\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 K ;switches, setting target;targets, setting switches;Integrated Development Environment, setting target switches}+{\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 + Open Watcom Integrated Development Environment:0047}{\keepn \f2 \fs28 Setting Target Switches \f2 \fs20 \par \pard \f2 \fs20 \tx360 \tx720 \tx1080 \tx1440 \tx1800 \tx2160 \tx2520 \tx2880 \tx3240 \tx3600 \tx3960 \tx4320 \tx4680 \tx5040 \tx5400 }
\par \li360 \f2\fi-360 \b To set switches for a target:\plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 
\par \f2\fi-360 \b (1)\plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 \b  Click once on the target window\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  for which you want to set switches.
\par 
\par The title bar of the selected window is highlighted.
\par 
\par \f2\fi-360 \b (2)\plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 \b  Choose Windows Linking Switches\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  from the Options menu.
\par 
\par or
\par 
\par \f2\fi-360 \b \plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 \b Choose Target Options\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  from the Targets menu and then choose Windows Linking Switches from the Target Options submenu.
\par 
\par The Windows Linking Switches dialog appears on the screen.  The switches you access from this dialog affect only the currently selected target.  There are four Switches dialogs, each one allowing you to set the following types of switches:
\par 
\par \li720 \fi-360\f1 \'b7\f2\tab Basic
\par \fi0 \fi-360\f1 \'b7\f2\tab Import, Export and Library
\par \fi0 \fi-360\f1 \'b7\f2\tab Advanced
\par \fi0 \fi-360\f1 \'b7\f2\tab Special
\par \fi0 \fi-360\f1 \'b7\f2\tab Resource
\par \fi0 \li360\fi0 
\par You can use the forward (>>) and backward (<<) buttons or the drop-down list to choose the set of switches you want to set.
\par 
\par \f4 \fs24 \qc \{bmc ide26.bmp\}\f2 \fs20 
\par \ql \i \b Figure 26.\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0 \i \b \b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0 \b  Use the Windows Linking Switches dialog to set command-line compiler and linker options.\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0 
\par 
\par The first dialog allows you to choose the debugging information you want to see.  Remember, if the option Use Development Switches is enabled in the Target Options submenu of the Targets menu, the Integrated Development Environment automatically sets switches to include all debugging information.  You can change this setting by selecting Use Release Switches from the Target Options submenu under the Targets menu or choosing specific switches in the Switches dialogs.
\par 
\par Select the\b  CopyRel\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  button in the Switches dialog to copy the set of Release switches to the current dialog.  You can then make further changes, as required.  This button is available only if the Development switches are selected in the Setup Target submenu under the Targets menu.
\par 
\par Select the\b  CopyDev\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  button in the Switches dialog to copy the set of Development switches to the current dialog.  You can then make further changes, as required.  This button is available only if the Release switches are selected in the Target Options submenu under the Targets menu.
\par 
\par Select the Reset button to reset the switches to the default Release or Development switches, as selected in the Target Options submenu under the Targets menu.
\par 
\par \f2\fi-360 \b (3)\plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 \b  Click on OK\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  when you are finished setting switches for the selected target.
\par 
\par The Switches dialog closes and you return to the Open Watcom IDE Project window.
\par \pard \page #{\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 # Setting_Source_File_Switches}${\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 $ Setting Source File Switches}!{\footnote ! ChangeButtonBinding( "btn_up", "JumpId( `ide.hlp', `Setting_Target_and_Source_File_Switches')") }K{\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 K ;switches, setting source file;source files, setting switches;Integrated Development Environment, setting source file switches}+{\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 + Open Watcom Integrated Development Environment:0048}{\keepn \f2 \fs28 Setting Source File Switches \f2 \fs20 \par \pard \f2 \fs20 \tx360 \tx720 \tx1080 \tx1440 \tx1800 \tx2160 \tx2520 \tx2880 \tx3240 \tx3600 \tx3960 \tx4320 \tx4680 \tx5040 \tx5400 }
\par The Switches function in the Source Options submenu of the Sources menu enables you to set switches for the compiler that is associated with the selected source file or source file group.
\par 
\par \li360 \f2\fi-360 \b To set switches for a source file group:\plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 
\par \f2\fi-360 \b (1)\plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 \b  Click once on the target window\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  that contains the relevant source files.
\par 
\par \f2\fi-360 \b (2)\plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 \b  Choose the appropriate item\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  from the Options menu.
\par 
\par For example, to set switches for all ".c" files in the selected target, choose C Compiler Switches from the Options menu.
\par 
\par The Compiler Switches dialog appears on the screen.  The switches you access from this dialog affect only the currently selected source file or source file group.  There are eleven Switches dialogs for Open Watcom C/C++, each one allowing you to set the following types of switches:
\par 
\par \li720 \fi-360\f1 \'b7\f2\tab File Option Switches
\par \fi0 \fi-360\f1 \'b7\f2\tab Diagnostics Switches
\par \fi0 \fi-360\f1 \'b7\f2\tab Source Switches
\par \fi0 \fi-360\f1 \'b7\f2\tab Miscellaneous Switches
\par \fi0 \fi-360\f1 \'b7\f2\tab Optimization Switches
\par \fi0 \fi-360\f1 \'b7\f2\tab Debugging Switches
\par \fi0 \fi-360\f1 \'b7\f2\tab CodeGen Strategy Switches
\par \fi0 \fi-360\f1 \'b7\f2\tab Register Usage Switches
\par \fi0 \fi-360\f1 \'b7\f2\tab CodeGen Option Switches
\par \fi0 \fi-360\f1 \'b7\f2\tab Memory Model and Processor Switches
\par \fi0 \fi-360\f1 \'b7\f2\tab C or C++ Compiler Switches
\par \fi0 \li360\fi0 
\par There are ten Switches dialogs for Open Watcom FORTRAN 77.
\par 
\par \li720 \fi-360\f1 \'b7\f2\tab File Option Switches
\par \fi0 \fi-360\f1 \'b7\f2\tab Compile-time Diagnostic Switches
\par \fi0 \fi-360\f1 \'b7\f2\tab Run-time Diagnostic Switches
\par \fi0 \fi-360\f1 \'b7\f2\tab Debugging Switches
\par \fi0 \fi-360\f1 \'b7\f2\tab Code Generation Strategy Switches
\par \fi0 \fi-360\f1 \'b7\f2\tab Code Generation Option Switches
\par \fi0 \fi-360\f1 \'b7\f2\tab Register Usage Switches
\par \fi0 \fi-360\f1 \'b7\f2\tab Optimization Switches
\par \fi0 \fi-360\f1 \'b7\f2\tab Application Type Switches
\par \fi0 \fi-360\f1 \'b7\f2\tab Miscellaneous Switches
\par \fi0 \li360\fi0 
\par You can use the forward (>>) and backward (<<) buttons or the drop-down list to choose the set of switches you want to set.
\par 
\par \f4 \fs24 \qc \{bmc ide27.bmp\}\f2 \fs20 
\par \ql \i \b Figure 27.\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0 \i \b \b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0 \b  Use the C Compiler Switches dialog to set switches for the compiler associated with the selected source file(s).\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0 
\par 
\par The first of several compiler switch dialogs appears.  Of note is the sixth screen, Debugging Switches, which allows you to choose the debugging information you want to see.  Remember, if the option Use Development Switches is enabled in the Target Options submenu of the Targets menu, the Integrated Development Environment automatically sets switches to include all debugging information.  You can change this setting by selecting Use Release Switches from the Target Options submenu under the Targets menu or choosing specific switches in the Switches dialogs.
\par 
\par Select the\b  CopyRel\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  button in the Switches dialog to copy the set of Release switches to the current dialog.  You can then make further changes, as required.  This button is available only if the Development switches are selected in the Target Options submenu under the Targets menu.
\par 
\par Select the\b  CopyDev\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  button in the Switches dialog to copy the set of Development switches to the current dialog.  You can then make further changes, as required.  This button is available only if the Release switches are selected in the Target Options submenu under the Targets menu.
\par 
\par Select the Reset button to reset the switches to the default Release or Development switches, as selected in the Target Options submenu under the Targets menu.
\par 
\par \f2\fi-360 \b (3)\plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 \b  Click on OK\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  when you are finished setting switches for the selected source file or group of source files.
\par 
\par The Switches dialog closes and you return to the Open Watcom IDE Project window.
\par \li0\fi0 
\par \li360 \f2\fi-360 \b To set switches for a specific source file:\plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 
\par \f2\fi-360 \b (1)\plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 \b  Click once on the source file\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  for which you want to set switches.
\par 
\par The selected source file is highlighted.
\par 
\par \f2\fi-360 \b (2)\plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 \b  Choose Source Options\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  from the Sources menu.
\par 
\par A submenu for the Source Options menu appears on the screen.
\par 
\par \f2\fi-360 \b (3)\plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 \b  Choose the appropriate item\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  from the Source Options submenu.
\par 
\par For example, if the selected source file has a ".c" extension, choose C Compiler Switches from the Options menu.
\par 
\par The Compiler Switches dialog appears on the screen.  The switches you access from this dialog affect only the currently selected source file.  There are eleven Switches dialogs for Open Watcom C/C++, each one allowing you to set the following types of switches:
\par 
\par \li720 \fi-360\f1 \'b7\f2\tab File Option Switches
\par \fi0 \fi-360\f1 \'b7\f2\tab Diagnostics Switches
\par \fi0 \fi-360\f1 \'b7\f2\tab Source Switches
\par \fi0 \fi-360\f1 \'b7\f2\tab Miscellaneous Switches
\par \fi0 \fi-360\f1 \'b7\f2\tab Optimization Switches
\par \fi0 \fi-360\f1 \'b7\f2\tab Debugging Switches
\par \fi0 \fi-360\f1 \'b7\f2\tab CodeGen Strategy Switches
\par \fi0 \fi-360\f1 \'b7\f2\tab Register Usage Switches
\par \fi0 \fi-360\f1 \'b7\f2\tab CodeGen Option Switches
\par \fi0 \fi-360\f1 \'b7\f2\tab Memory Model and Processor Switches
\par \fi0 \fi-360\f1 \'b7\f2\tab C or C++ Compiler Switches
\par \fi0 \li360\fi0 
\par There are ten Switches dialogs for Open Watcom FORTRAN 77.
\par 
\par \li720 \fi-360\f1 \'b7\f2\tab File Option Switches
\par \fi0 \fi-360\f1 \'b7\f2\tab Compile-time Diagnostic Switches
\par \fi0 \fi-360\f1 \'b7\f2\tab Run-time Diagnostic Switches
\par \fi0 \fi-360\f1 \'b7\f2\tab Debugging Switches
\par \fi0 \fi-360\f1 \'b7\f2\tab Code Generation Strategy Switches
\par \fi0 \fi-360\f1 \'b7\f2\tab Code Generation Option Switches
\par \fi0 \fi-360\f1 \'b7\f2\tab Register Usage Switches
\par \fi0 \fi-360\f1 \'b7\f2\tab Optimization Switches
\par \fi0 \fi-360\f1 \'b7\f2\tab Application Type Switches
\par \fi0 \fi-360\f1 \'b7\f2\tab Miscellaneous Switches
\par \fi0 \li360\fi0 
\par You can use the forward (>>) and backward (<<) buttons or the drop-down list to choose the set of switches you want to set.
\par 
\par \f4 \fs24 \qc \{bmc ide28.bmp\}\f2 \fs20 
\par \ql \i \b Figure 28.\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0 \i \b \b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0 \b  Use the C Compiler Switches dialog to set switches for the compiler associated with the selected source file(s).\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0 
\par 
\par The first of several compiler switch dialogs appears.  Of note is the sixth screen, Debugging Switches, which allows you to choose the debugging information you want to see.  Remember, if the option Use Development Switches is enabled in the Target Options submenu of the Targets menu, the Integrated Development Environment automatically sets switches to include all debugging information.  You can change this setting by selecting Use Release Switches from the Target Options submenu under the Targets menu or choosing specific switches in the Switches dialogs.
\par 
\par Select the\b  CopyRel\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  button in the Switches dialog to copy the set of Release switches to the current dialog.  You can then make further changes, as required.  This button is available only if the Development switches are selected in the Target Options submenu under the Targets menu.
\par 
\par Select the\b  CopyDev\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  button in the Switches dialog to copy the set of Development switches to the current dialog.  You can then make further changes, as required.  This button is available only if the Release switches are selected in the Target Options submenu under the Targets menu.
\par 
\par Select the Reset button to reset the switches to the default Release or Development switches, as selected in the Target Options submenu under the Targets menu.
\par 
\par \f2\fi-360 \b (4)\plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 \b  Click on OK\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  when you are finished setting switches for the selected source file or group of source files.
\par 
\par The Switches dialog closes and you return to the Open Watcom IDE Project window.
\par \pard \page #{\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 # Using_the_Autodepend_Switch}${\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 $ Using the Autodepend Switch}!{\footnote ! ChangeButtonBinding( "btn_up", "JumpId( `ide.hlp', `The_IDE_Switches')") }K{\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 K ;switches, autodepend;Integrated Development Environment, autodepend switch}+{\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 + Open Watcom Integrated Development Environment:0049}{\keepn \f2 \fs28 Using the Autodepend Switch \f2 \fs20 \par \pard \f2 \fs20 \tx360 \tx720 \tx1080 \tx1440 \tx1800 \tx2160 \tx2520 \tx2880 \tx3240 \tx3600 \tx3960 \tx4320 \tx4680 \tx5040 \tx5400 }
\par The Use Autodepend switch in the Target Options submenu of the Targets menu keeps track of updated source files.  Once a source is modified, the IDE remembers that it needs to be recompiled the next time you do a make.  By using the Use Autodepend switch, the IDE also keeps track of changes made to source files on which other source files depend.
\par 
\par For example, let's say that your current project has a source file,\f4 \fs24  source.cpp,\f2 \fs20  that depends on a header file,\f4 \fs24  source.hpp.\f2 \fs20   If the Use Autodepend feature is not enabled and changes are made to\f4 \fs24  source.hpp\f2 \fs20  after\f4 \fs24  source.cpp\f2 \fs20  in your current project has been compiled, the IDE does not recognize that your current source file is now out of date.  This means that the next time you do a make, the IDE will not compile\f4 \fs24  source.cpp.\f2 \fs20   The Use Autodepend feature tells the IDE to compare the date and time stamps on all of the dependent source files in addition to the source files in the current project and their corresponding object files, so the IDE recognizes when your current source file needs to be recompiled.
\par 
\par \li360 \f2\fi-360 \b To set the Use Autodepend switch:\plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 
\par \f2\fi-360 \b (1)\plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 \b  Choose Target Options\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  from the Targets menu.
\par 
\par A submenu appears from the Target Options menu.  A check mark beside the menu option indicates that it is enabled.
\par 
\par \f2\fi-360 \b (2)\plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 \b  Select the Use Autodepend option\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  from the Target Options submenu.
\par 
\par If the option was enabled when you selected it, you have disabled the Use Autodepend feature.  If the option had no check mark when you selected it, you have enabled the Use Autodepend feature.
\par \pard \page #{\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 # Configuring_the_Integrated_Development_Environment}${\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 $ Configuring the Integrated Development Environment}!{\footnote ! ChangeButtonBinding( "btn_up", "JumpId( `ide.hlp', `Open_Watcom_Integrated_Development_Environment')") }K{\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 K ;customizing the IDE;Integrated Development Environment, customization;configuring the IDE;Integrated Development Environment, configuration}+{\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 + Open Watcom Integrated Development Environment:0050}{\keepn \f2 \fs28 Configuring the Integrated Development Environment \f2 \fs20 \par \pard \f2 \fs20 \tx360 \tx720 \tx1080 \tx1440 \tx1800 \tx2160 \tx2520 \tx2880 \tx3240 \tx3600 \tx3960 \tx4320 \tx4680 \tx5040 \tx5400 }
\par The Integrated Development Environment can be reconfigured to some extent.  You can customize the following features.
\par 
\par \li360 \fi-360\f1 \'b7\f2\tab Show or hide the Toolbar
\par \fi0 \fi-360\f1 \'b7\f2\tab Show or hide the Status Bar
\par \fi0 \fi-360\f1 \'b7\f2\tab Enable or disable automatic updates of file status in the target windows
\par \fi0 \fi-360\f1 \'b7\f2\tab Select your own text editor
\par \fi0 \fi-360\f1 \'b7\f2\tab Select a source revision control system
\par \fi0 \li0\fi0 
\par The next few sections describe these features.
\par \pard \page #{\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 # Show_Toolbar}${\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 $ Show Toolbar}!{\footnote ! ChangeButtonBinding( "btn_up", "JumpId( `ide.hlp', `Configuring_the_Integrated_Development_Environment')") }K{\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 K ;show tool bar}+{\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 + Open Watcom Integrated Development Environment:0051}{\keepn \f2 \fs28 Show Toolbar \f2 \fs20 \par \pard \f2 \fs20 \tx360 \tx720 \tx1080 \tx1440 \tx1800 \tx2160 \tx2520 \tx2880 \tx3240 \tx3600 \tx3960 \tx4320 \tx4680 \tx5040 \tx5400 }
\par The Integrated Development Environment Toolbar that appears under the Menu Bar can be removed from or added to the IDE window.
\par 
\par \li360 \f2\fi-360 \b To add or remove the toolbar:\plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 
\par \f2\fi-360 \b (1)\plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 \b  Choose File\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  from the menu bar.
\par 
\par A checkmark beside Show Tool Bar indicates that the toolbar is present on the screen.
\par 
\par \f2\fi-360 \b (2)\plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 \b  Select Show Tool Bar.\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0 
\par 
\par The Show Tool Bar state will be toggled.  If the toolbar was on the screen, it will disappear.  If the toolbar was not on the screen, it will reappear.
\par \pard \page #{\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 # Show_Status_Bar}${\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 $ Show Status Bar}!{\footnote ! ChangeButtonBinding( "btn_up", "JumpId( `ide.hlp', `Configuring_the_Integrated_Development_Environment')") }K{\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 K ;show status bar}+{\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 + Open Watcom Integrated Development Environment:0052}{\keepn \f2 \fs28 Show Status Bar \f2 \fs20 \par \pard \f2 \fs20 \tx360 \tx720 \tx1080 \tx1440 \tx1800 \tx2160 \tx2520 \tx2880 \tx3240 \tx3600 \tx3960 \tx4320 \tx4680 \tx5040 \tx5400 }
\par The Integrated Development Environment Status Bar that appears at the bottom of the IDE window can be removed from or added to the screen.
\par 
\par \li360 \f2\fi-360 \b To add or remove the status bar:\plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 
\par \f2\fi-360 \b (1)\plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 \b  Choose File\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  from the menu bar.
\par 
\par A checkmark beside Show Status Bar indicates that the status bar is present on the screen.
\par 
\par \f2\fi-360 \b (2)\plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 \b  Select Show Status Bar.\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0 
\par 
\par The Show Status Bar state will be toggled.  If the status bar was on the screen, it will disappear.  If the status bar was not on the screen, it will reappear.
\par \pard \page #{\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 # Auto_Refresh}${\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 $ Auto Refresh}!{\footnote ! ChangeButtonBinding( "btn_up", "JumpId( `ide.hlp', `Configuring_the_Integrated_Development_Environment')") }K{\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 K ;auto refresh}+{\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 + Open Watcom Integrated Development Environment:0053}{\keepn \f2 \fs28 Auto Refresh \f2 \fs20 \par \pard \f2 \fs20 \tx360 \tx720 \tx1080 \tx1440 \tx1800 \tx2160 \tx2520 \tx2880 \tx3240 \tx3600 \tx3960 \tx4320 \tx4680 \tx5040 \tx5400 }
\par The IDE will automatically update the target windows with the state of all files when this option is enabled.
\par 
\par \li360 \f2\fi-360 \b To enable or disable auto refresh:\plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 
\par \f2\fi-360 \b (1)\plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 \b  Choose File\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  from the menu bar.
\par 
\par A checkmark beside Auto Refresh indicates that auto refresh is enabled.
\par 
\par \f2\fi-360 \b (2)\plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 \b  Select Auto Refresh.\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0 
\par 
\par The Auto Refresh state will be toggled.  When Auto Refresh is disabled, it may be necessary to select Refresh from the Window menu whenever the target window status becomes out of date.
\par \pard \page #{\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 # Selecting_Your_Own_Text_Editor}${\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 $ Selecting Your Own Text Editor}!{\footnote ! ChangeButtonBinding( "btn_up", "JumpId( `ide.hlp', `Configuring_the_Integrated_Development_Environment')") }K{\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 K ;set text editor}+{\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 + Open Watcom Integrated Development Environment:0054}{\keepn \f2 \fs28 Selecting Your Own Text Editor \f2 \fs20 \par \pard \f2 \fs20 \tx360 \tx720 \tx1080 \tx1440 \tx1800 \tx2160 \tx2520 \tx2880 \tx3240 \tx3600 \tx3960 \tx4320 \tx4680 \tx5040 \tx5400 }
\par You can use your own favourite text editor from within the Integrated Development Environment.
\par 
\par \li360 \f2\fi-360 \b To select your own text editor:\plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 
\par \f2\fi-360 \b (1)\plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 \b  Choose Set Text Editor\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  from the File menu.
\par 
\par The Set Text Editor dialog appears.  You can enter the name of the text editor in the first field.  You must also indicate whether the text editor is an executable file or a Dynamic Link Library (DLL).  If the editor is an executable file (rather than a DLL), then you can enter an argument line in the second field.  The argument line will be supplied to the editor whenever it is started by the IDE.  The argument line can include any of three different macros which will be filled in by the IDE.  The macros are:
\par 
\par \li720 \f2\fi-360 \b %f\plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 The name of the file to be edited.
\par 
\par \f2\fi-360 \b %r\plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 The row in the file at which to position the cursor.  If the editor is invoked from a diagnostic message which contains a line number then the row value is extracted from the message; otherwise the row value is 1.
\par 
\par \f2\fi-360 \b %c\plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 The column in the file at which to position the cursor.  If the editor is invoked from a diagnostic message which contains a column number then the column value is extracted from the message; otherwise the column value is 1.
\par \li360\fi0 
\par For example, if the editor argument line that you specified was:
\par 
\par \f4 \fs24       \f2 \fs20 
\par \f4 \fs24      file='%f' row='%r' col='%c'\f2 \fs20 
\par 
\par and you double click on an error message in the Log window that names the file\f4 \fs24  foobar.c\f2 \fs20  with an error at line 215 and column 31, then the argument line that is passed to your editor is:
\par 
\par \f4 \fs24       \f2 \fs20 
\par \f4 \fs24      file='foobar.c' row='215' col='31'\f2 \fs20 
\par 
\par This flexibility allows you to specify the name of the file to edit and the row and/or column at which to position the text cursor.  If no row or column is available, then the IDE will supply the value of 1 as a default.
\par 
\par \f2\fi-360 \b (2)\plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 \b  Select OK\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  when you wish to confirm the selection of a new editor.
\par 
\par or
\par 
\par \f2\fi-360 \b \plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 \b Select Cancel\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  when you wish to cancel the selection of a new editor.
\par 
\par or
\par 
\par \f2\fi-360 \b \plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 \b Select Default\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  when you wish to restore the default editor selection and then select OK or Cancel.
\par \pard \page #{\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 # Selecting_a_Source_Revision_Control_System}${\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 $ Selecting a Source Revision Control System}!{\footnote ! ChangeButtonBinding( "btn_up", "JumpId( `ide.hlp', `Configuring_the_Integrated_Development_Environment')") }K{\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 K ;checkin source;checkout source;Perforce;Object Cycle;generic RCS;PVCS;MKS SI;MKS RCS;RCS;set source control}+{\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 + Open Watcom Integrated Development Environment:0055}{\keepn \f2 \fs28 Selecting a Source Revision Control System \f2 \fs20 \par \pard \f2 \fs20 \tx360 \tx720 \tx1080 \tx1440 \tx1800 \tx2160 \tx2520 \tx2880 \tx3240 \tx3600 \tx3960 \tx4320 \tx4680 \tx5040 \tx5400 }
\par You can use a source code Revision Control System (RCS) in conjunction with the Integrated Development Environment.
\par 
\par \li360 \f2\fi-360 \b To select a revision control system\plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 
\par \f2\fi-360 \b (1)\plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 \b  Choose Set Source Control\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  from the File menu.
\par 
\par A number of options are presented:
\par 
\par \li720 \f2\fi-360 \b MKS RCS\plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 Mortice Kern Systems Revision Control System
\par 
\par \f2\fi-360 \b MKS SI\plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 Mortice Kern Systems Source Integrity
\par 
\par \f2\fi-360 \b PVCS\plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 Intersolv Project Version Control System
\par 
\par \f2\fi-360 \b Other\plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 Any other flavour of revision control system (default)
\par 
\par \f2\fi-360 \b None\plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 No revision control system
\par 
\par \f2\fi-360 \b Object Cycle\plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 Powersoft's Object Cycle
\par 
\par \f2\fi-360 \b Perforce\plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 Perforce
\par \li360\fi0 
\par \li0\fi0 
\par When you select a source revision control system, the Checkout Source and Checkin Source items in the Sources menu are enabled.  The Checkout Source and Checkin Source items spawn batch/command files or dialogs corresponding to your selection of revision control system.  The batch/command files are located in the \\WATCOM\\BINW and \\WATCOM\\BINP directories.  These files can be modified to suit your environment.
\par 
\par \li360 \f2\fi-360 \b MKS RCS\plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 
\par \li720 \f2\fi-360 \b MKS_CO\plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 for checking out/locking files with MKS RCS
\par 
\par \f2\fi-360 \b MKS_CI\plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 for checking in/unlocking files with MKS RCS
\par \li360\fi0 
\par \f2\fi-360 \b MKS SI\plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 
\par \li720 \f2\fi-360 \b Checkout Dialog\plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 a check out dialog will appear on the screen when using MKS SI
\par 
\par \f2\fi-360 \b Checkin Dialog\plain\f2\fs20
