\par \pard \page #{\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 # Executing_Special_Commands}${\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 $ Executing Special Commands}!{\footnote ! ChangeButtonBinding( "btn_up", "JumpId( `ide.hlp', `Open_Watcom_Integrated_Development_Environment')") }K{\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 K ;after make;after;Integrated Development Environment, after make;before make;before;Integrated Development Environment, before make}+{\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 + Open Watcom Integrated Development Environment:0056}{\keepn \f2 \fs28 Executing Special Commands \f2 \fs20 \par \pard \f2 \fs20 \tx360 \tx720 \tx1080 \tx1440 \tx1800 \tx2160 \tx2520 \tx2880 \tx3240 \tx3600 \tx3960 \tx4320 \tx4680 \tx5040 \tx5400 }
\par Sometimes you may find it necessary to execute commands before and/or after performing a make.  The Open Watcom Integrated Development Environment provides Before and After commands in the File and Targets menus.  These commands enable you to perform actions immediately before or after making either the selected target or all targets for the current project.
\par 
\par \li360 \f2\fi-360 \b To set Before or After directives:\plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 
\par \f2\fi-360 \b (1)\plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 \b  Click once on the target\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  for which you want to set a before or after directive.
\par 
\par The title bar of the selected target is highlighted.
\par 
\par \f2\fi-360 \b (2)\plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 \b  Choose Target Options\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  from the Targets menu.
\par 
\par A submenu appears from the Target Options menu.
\par 
\par \f2\fi-360 \b (3)\plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 \b  Choose Execute Before or Execute After\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  from the Target Options submenu.
\par 
\par A Target Before or Target After dialog appears on your screen.
\par 
\par \li720 \f2\fi-360 \b Note:\plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 If you want to set before or after directives to be executed when you make all targets, select the before and after commands from the File menu.
\par \li360\fi0 
\par \f2\fi-360 \b (4)\plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 \b  Enter the command\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  you want to execute before or after the selected target is made.
\par 
\par \f4 \fs24 \qc \{bmc ide29.bmp\}\f2 \fs20 
\par \ql \i \b Figure 29.\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0 \i \b \b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0 \b  Enter execute commands in the Target Before or Target After dialogs.\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0 
\par 
\par \f2\fi-360 \b (5)\plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 \b  Click on OK\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  to continue.
\par 
\par The IDE stores the information you entered and closes the dialog.  When you next perform a make on the selected target, the IDE will execute the before or after directive you entered.
\par \pard \page #{\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 # Adding_Resources_to_a_Target}${\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 $ Adding Resources to a Target}!{\footnote ! ChangeButtonBinding( "btn_up", "JumpId( `ide.hlp', `Open_Watcom_Integrated_Development_Environment')") }K{\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 K ;resources, adding;Integrated Development Environment, adding resources}+{\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 + Open Watcom Integrated Development Environment:0057}{\keepn \f2 \fs28 Adding Resources to a Target \f2 \fs20 \par \pard \f2 \fs20 \tx360 \tx720 \tx1080 \tx1440 \tx1800 \tx2160 \tx2520 \tx2880 \tx3240 \tx3600 \tx3960 \tx4320 \tx4680 \tx5040 \tx5400 }
\par You may want to include resources in your target executable.  There are two ways of doing this in the IDE:  indirectly and directly.  The act of adding resources to a target can only be done for targets that support resources, such as Windows or OS/2 PM.
\par 
\par \li360 \f2\fi-360 \b To add a resource to a target using the indirect method:\plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 
\par \f2\fi-360 \b (1)\plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 \b  Create a new source file\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  with the extension ".rc".
\par 
\par \f2\fi-360 \b (2)\plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 \b  Click once on the new source file\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  to select it.
\par 
\par \f2\fi-360 \b (3)\plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 \b  Choose Edit Text\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  from the Sources menu to invoke the Open Watcom Editor.
\par 
\par The editor opens on your screen with an empty window.  You can now add the resource file text.
\par 
\par \f2\fi-360 \b (4)\plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 \b  Enter the resource statements\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  in the editor.
\par 
\par \f2\fi-360 \b (5)\plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 \b  Save your work and close the editor\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  when you are finished.
\par 
\par You return to the Open Watcom IDE Project window.  You can now compile the new source file.
\par 
\par \f2\fi-360 \b (6)\plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 \b  Click once on the new source file\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  to select it.
\par 
\par The selected source file is highlighted in the target window.
\par 
\par \f2\fi-360 \b (7)\plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 \b  Choose Make\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  from the Sources menu to compile the new source file.
\par 
\par The IDE compiles the selected source file.  To do this, the IDE invokes the Resource Compiler which creates a ".res" file.  The ".res" file will be automatically included in the target when it is linked.
\par \li0\fi0 
\par When you create the resource file directly, the source file contains the same information but it is stored in a different format.
\par 
\par \li360 \f2\fi-360 \b To add a resource to a target using the direct method:\plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 
\par \f2\fi-360 \b (1)\plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 \b  Create a new source file\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  with the extension ".res".
\par 
\par \f2\fi-360 \b (2)\plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 \b  Click once on the new source file\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  to select it.
\par 
\par \f2\fi-360 \b (3)\plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 \b  Choose Edit Resources\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  from the Sources menu to invoke the Open Watcom Resource Editor.
\par 
\par The editor opens on your screen with an empty window.  You can now create the resources.
\par 
\par \f2\fi-360 \b (4)\plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 \b  Create the resources.\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0 
\par 
\par \f2\fi-360 \b (5)\plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 \b  Save your work and close the editor\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  when you are finished.
\par 
\par You return to the Open Watcom IDE Project window.  You can now compile the new source file.
\par 
\par \f2\fi-360 \b (6)\plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 \b  Click once on the new source file\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  to select it.
\par 
\par The selected source file is highlighted in the target window.
\par 
\par \f2\fi-360 \b (7)\plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 \b  Choose Make\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  from the Sources menu to compile the new source file.
\par 
\par The IDE compiles the selected source file.  The IDE does not need to invoke the Resource Compiler to create a ".res" file because you created the ".res" file directly using the Resource Editor.  The ".res" file will be automatically included in the target when it is linked.
\par \pard \page #{\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 # Including_Libraries_in_a_Target}${\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 $ Including Libraries in a Target}!{\footnote ! ChangeButtonBinding( "btn_up", "JumpId( `ide.hlp', `Open_Watcom_Integrated_Development_Environment')") }K{\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 K ;libraries, adding;Integrated Development Environment, adding libraries}+{\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 + Open Watcom Integrated Development Environment:0058}{\keepn \f2 \fs28 Including Libraries in a Target \f2 \fs20 \par \pard \f2 \fs20 \tx360 \tx720 \tx1080 \tx1440 \tx1800 \tx2160 \tx2520 \tx2880 \tx3240 \tx3600 \tx3960 \tx4320 \tx4680 \tx5040 \tx5400 }
\par You use the same procedure to include static and DLL libraries in your target as you use to add any source file to a target.  The IDE includes the libraries when the target is linked.
\par 
\par \li360 \f2\fi-360 \b To create static and DLL libraries:\plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 
\par \f2\fi-360 \b (1)\plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 \b  Create a new target\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  entering the library name in the Target Name field and choosing the appropriate image type.
\par 
\par Clicking on OK on the New Target dialog closes the dialog and creates a new target with the specified name in the title bar.
\par 
\par \f2\fi-360 \b (2)\plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 \b  Add the desired source files,\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  new or existing, to the library you have created.
\par 
\par \li720 \f2\fi-360 \b Note:\plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 If you want one of your other targets to use that library, you must enter the library as a source file for that target.  When you enter the library as a source file you must give it the extension ".lib", regardless of whether the library is a static or DLL library.  For example, if your library is entitled Target A and you want your existing Target B to use that library, add Target A as a source file on Target B with the extension ".lib".
\par \li360\fi0 
\par \pard \page #{\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 # Targets_and_Image_Types_Supported_by_the_IDE}${\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 $ Targets and Image Types Supported by the IDE}!{\footnote ! ChangeButtonBinding( "btn_up", "JumpId( `ide.hlp', `Open_Watcom_Integrated_Development_Environment')") }K{\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 K ;targets}+{\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 + Open Watcom Integrated Development Environment:0059}{\keepn \f2 \fs28 Targets and Image Types Supported by the IDE \f2 \fs20 \par \pard \f2 \fs20 \tx360 \tx720 \tx1080 \tx1440 \tx1800 \tx2160 \tx2520 \tx2880 \tx3240 \tx3600 \tx3960 \tx4320 \tx4680 \tx5040 \tx5400 }
