\par \pard \page #{\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 # Invoking_Other_Applications_from_the_IDE}${\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 $ Invoking Other Applications from the IDE}!{\footnote ! ChangeButtonBinding( "btn_up", "JumpId( `ide.hlp', `Open_Watcom_Integrated_Development_Environment')") }K{\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 K ;right button;mouse, right button;invoking tools;Integrated Development Environment, invoking tools}+{\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 + Open Watcom Integrated Development Environment:0068}{\keepn \f2 \fs28 Invoking Other Applications from the IDE \f2 \fs20 \par \pard \f2 \fs20 \tx360 \tx720 \tx1080 \tx1440 \tx1800 \tx2160 \tx2520 \tx2880 \tx3240 \tx3600 \tx3960 \tx4320 \tx4680 \tx5040 \tx5400 }
\par When using the Open Watcom Integrated Development Environment, there are several other applications you may want to use.  To save you the frustration of having to constantly exit the IDE and start up a new application, we have made it possible to invoke other applications from within the Integrated Development Environment.  Following are the applications you can invoke from the IDE:
\par 
\par \li360 \fi-360\f1 \'b7\f2\tab Browser
\par \fi0 \fi-360\f1 \'b7\f2\tab Open Watcom Debugger
\par \fi0 \fi-360\f1 \'b7\f2\tab Open Watcom Execution Sampler
\par \fi0 \fi-360\f1 \'b7\f2\tab Open Watcom Execution Profiler
\par \fi0 \fi-360\f1 \'b7\f2\tab Open Watcom Disassembler
\par \fi0 \fi-360\f1 \'b7\f2\tab Open Watcom Editor (text editor)
\par \fi0 \fi-360\f1 \'b7\f2\tab Open Watcom Resource Editor
\par \fi0 \fi-360\f1 \'b7\f2\tab Open Watcom Zoom
\par \fi0 \fi-360\f1 \'b7\f2\tab Open Watcom Heap Walker
\par \fi0 \fi-360\f1 \'b7\f2\tab Spy
\par \fi0 \fi-360\f1 \'b7\f2\tab DDE Spy
\par \fi0 \li0\fi0 
\par You invoke these applications by making a selection from the toolbar or various menus in the IDE.  The Actions menu contains some of the applications.  You can find others in the Targets and Sources menus.  The menu options that appear vary, depending on the contents of the current project.  The applications are available in the menu items only when appropriate.  For example, if the currently selected target has no source files, no applications appear in the Sources menu.
\par 
\par \i \b Hint:\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0   An alternate way to view the contents of the Targets and Sources menu is to click the right mouse button while your cursor is positioned in the project window.  If your cursor is positioned over a target, the Targets menu appears.  If your cursor is positioned over a source file, the Sources menu appears.
\par 
\par Refer to the appropriate sections in this manual for detailed information on using the various applications.
\par \pard \page #{\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 # Invoking_DOS_Applications_from_the_IDE_under_OSD2}${\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 $ Invoking DOS Applications from the IDE under OS/2}!{\footnote ! ChangeButtonBinding( "btn_up", "JumpId( `ide.hlp', `Open_Watcom_Integrated_Development_Environment')") }K{\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 K ;IDE_DOS_SETTINGS environment variable;environment variables, IDE_DOS_SETTINGS;WIN-OS/2 settings;DOS settings}+{\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 + Open Watcom Integrated Development Environment:0069}{\keepn \f2 \fs28 Invoking DOS Applications from the IDE under OS/2 \f2 \fs20 \par \pard \f2 \fs20 \tx360 \tx720 \tx1080 \tx1440 \tx1800 \tx2160 \tx2520 \tx2880 \tx3240 \tx3600 \tx3960 \tx4320 \tx4680 \tx5040 \tx5400 }
\par There are occasions where you may wish to change the default DOS or WIN-OS/2 settings when running a DOS/Windows application from the IDE.  The mechanism which we provide is through the use of a special\b  IDE_DOS_SETTINGS\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  environment variable.  For example, to set the DPMI memory limit to 16 and to enable DOS BREAK, you can set the environment variable as follows:
\par 
\par \b Example:\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0 
\par 
\par \f4 \fs24      set ide_dos_settings=dpmi_memory_limit#16;dos_break#1\f2 \fs20 
\par 
\par Due to the command processor limitations, the "#" character is used in place of the "=".  The above command is equivalent to configuring a DOS session with DOS settings as follows:
\par 
\par \f4 \fs24       \f2 \fs20 
\par \f4 \fs24      Variable            Value\f2 \fs20 
\par \f4 \fs24      DPMI_MEMORY_LIMIT   16\f2 \fs20 
\par \f4 \fs24      DOS_BREAK           ON\f2 \fs20 
\par \pard \page #{\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 # Using_the_Information_of_the_IDE_from_outside}${\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 $ Using the Information of the IDE from outside}!{\footnote ! ChangeButtonBinding( "btn_up", "JumpId( `ide.hlp', `Open_Watcom_Integrated_Development_Environment')") }K{\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 K ;IDE2MAKE, Utility;IDE, make}+{\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 + Open Watcom Integrated Development Environment:0070}{\keepn \f2 \fs28 Using the Information of the IDE from outside \f2 \fs20 \par \pard \f2 \fs20 \tx360 \tx720 \tx1080 \tx1440 \tx1800 \tx2160 \tx2520 \tx2880 \tx3240 \tx3600 \tx3960 \tx4320 \tx4680 \tx5040 \tx5400 }
\par The batch utility IDE2MAKE generates .mk and .mk1 makefiles for use with Open Watcom Make from the IDE .wpj and .tgt files.  See IDE2MAKE in Open Watcom Tools User's Guide.
\par 
